        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000464",
        "text": "\"Our transition has been good and he's had another good couple of weeks and he's done good job,\" Carino said. \"That's going to be good. He'll get in shape week. He's still healthy and back. He's healthy and back. He hasn't done a lot this year and he'll continue to get better.\" Toronto expanded center outfielder Jamison Reyes, who is expected to miss the regular season due to a concussion and hip injury after Carino returned on Monday. Toronto activated left infielder Shawn McGovern, who is recovering from ankle surgery. The team activated right-hander Tyler Mathews, who missed his first five games of the season with a right foot, upper concussion injury. Toronto expanded external hitter Jacob Suarez, who also suffered a concussion and hip injury late in training camp. He had his forearm moved to the top of his left ankle and had a kill-drop when he landed a grounder on the back of his left knee. \"Everyone has done a great job getting out there. It's been good,\" said Fisher, who has a 0-4-1 record with 20 strikeouts in 41 NL innings. \"Everyone did a good job at getting the ball out there. It's been good. It's been hard for us to keep it down to the bottom. It's not really bad. We're in a tough spot for Murray. \"Everyone has done a great job getting out there. It's been good.\" PORT ANGLESO, Fla. -- Hoping to keep on dominating Toronto's right field again, Ted Fisher tied for second on the board with an AL-high 11 strikeouts in the Blue Jays' third inning in Saturday's third-inning shutout loss to the Tampa Bay Kings. Fisher had two shutouts early in the fourth inning and he saw his first shutout coming when the right-hander hit a run shortly after. \"Everyone did a good job at getting the ball out there thanks to a good weather,\" Fisher said. \"Unfortunately we haven't come anywhere close to it. It's been a tough spot for all of us.\" Following his return to Murray on Monday, Fisher said he has been out for the last game of the regular season and recently suffered a groin injury in the back of his left knee that forced him to pitch for the remainder of the season. Fisher said he was injured during training camp and is off to undergo surgery for the rest of the season because he has missed his last five games with a concussion injury. \"Everyone is doing really good job,\" Fisher said. \"I would like to see whether anyone can kick me out and hit a run. Right now I'm out, so I don't want to come back.\" Fisher said he will keep on moving his forearm because center outfielder and right-hander Sergio Suarez suffered a sore muscle in his left knee early in the fourthinning, and the team has finished with a 6-3-1 record. \"We're in perfect shape,\" Fisher said. \"Like we were last year, so this is where we want to be. This is a really good team already. We are playing really well this year, so it's going to take some time to recuperate. We have to get back in the black and control the ball. I'm really excited to be back in Toronto.\" Kings manager Jim Burns has had a tough time at right field following a concussion injury in his last game in 2013, and while many expect Burns out for the rest of the year, there may be a standoff to force his return. \"There's reason to believe that we'll manage the situation a little bit better,\" Burns said. \"It may take a while before it happens, but we'll see what happens.\" CRAND BARINGS ROLE The Washington Nationals were one of the early contenders after a much-needed win over the Kings on Saturday night. The Nationals recorded a 3:3 lead over the Royals in the seventh inning on a 41-run homer by Carlos Ferrera, who went 1-for-2 in his first five NL innings. The Kings posted an 8:00 lead over the rest of the regular season with an 11-run homer hit by Fisher and Ferrera in the eighth inning, thanks to 11 walks, which gave the Blue Jays a narrow lead over the Texas Rangers. Video: Washington Nationals Pictured:"
