        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000681",
        "text": "Later this month, Sony announced their first new playable for the Xbox One and PlayStation 4 platforms. This marks their first announcement of the company's new i-Disable Shooter Mode, a name console developers haven't officially pointed out yet. Around the time, Skywalker Digital further announced a new 'Disable Shooter Mode' feature on the PlayStation 4. Also last month, Sony announced the new 'Sleep Up' i-Disable Shooter Mode in its Fusion system. The new playable has come close to two years after being announced, so we'll have to wait three more years to get the full details. We don't know when the new feature will arrive on other platforms, as it was first shown on the Xbox One and the PS a few weeks ago. In a blog post last week, Sony revealed that the new feature was coming to the Xbox One, PlayStation 4 and PlayStation 4. You can find our review of the new playable, 'iDisable Shooter Mode,' at Sony's Mode Breaks 2013 blog. Image copyright Reuters Image caption Helicz interns have played a key role in boosting Helicz's work network The firm is expected to add more than 100,000 jobs. The number of internships held by Helicz interns has nearly doubled in the last two years - to 20,000 by the first month of last year, according to data provided by the Managing Professional Jobs Authority. About 5,000 workers at Helicz Systems Ltd were on leave in 2015, an increase from about 6,000 in 2012. The departure marks a 34.1% increase in jobs in the last three months. The internships held by the firm's chief technology officer have nearly doubled to 49,039, accounting for roughly 25% (6.9%) of the total internships held by Helicz workers in 2015 compared to about half of the internships held in 2012. Image caption Half of Helicz workers went on leave In February early this year, Ms Hall accepted the position of chief executive of Angus Systems from her Princeton Park office. The job also included as the head of software development and managemental services. Using quarterly employment statistics, Ms Hall expects Helicz to add more than 100,000 jobs over the next 10 years. Image copyright Reuters Image caption Ms Hall talks about boosting Helicz's work network \"I'm convinced we need to hire some of the best and greatest talent,\" she said. \"When I work for the company, I'm a more productive work manager because I can engage with colleagues and adapt to the company's strengths.\" Image copyright Reuters Image caption Quarter employment statistics show that half of last year's Helicz workers went on leave The data provided by the Managing Professional Jobss Authority suggests Helicz interns contributed a spike in jobs in the first three months of 2015 compared to the first three months of last year. The bulk of Helicz's workforce is made up of assistant interns who go through internship screenings, where they are subsequently accepted. These can include an engineering lecturer, a technical engineer, engineer and designer. Helicz vice president of software development Josh Dong said: \"Not only we have a robust work network covering a wide range of areas, but also we need to reach out to applicants in a clear and transparent manner.\" LONDON, MAY 24 (Reuters) - The increase in sea ice is attributed to a warming global climate since the start of one century, a new study using greenhouse gases measurement software at the University of Bradley shows on Saturday. The Bradley study, which will soon be published in the UK's Journal of Meteorology, finds that the amount of ice off the mainland United States has surged in the 21st century faster than any rate observed since the start of one century. Compared with other studies in that area, increasing the level of ice over time is not much of a factor. The ice today covers off the coast by about 1.5 kilometers (3.6 miles), which contribute to many factors including climate changes in the Arctic and rising temperatures in the Arctic. \"The correlation with warming levels off the Arctic is well established,\" says study author Daniel Chapman of the UK's Department of Astronomy and Geophemical Sciences. \"Climate changes in the Arctic have also been strongly linked to an increased level of sea ice coverage.\" The researchers used models to see if there are any signs that climate changes in the Arctic Sea are responsible for the subsequent rise in the ice. To identify which factors are contributing to the increase in sea ice over time, the researchers used an ATT Nirma-class atmospheric current detector to run the models with up to 90% accuracy.."
