      {
        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000834",
        "text": "Nation will air on ABC's Jane & John from 10 a.m. to 1:30 p.m. on Sunday, January 20, 2016. Tickets for Sunday's show will go on sale for $20, following the premiere date. Click here for an update to the time to book. WASHINGTON – Net income among Americans increased from 22 percent in December in 2015 to $11.34 trillion in the first quarter of 2016, representing the largest annual income growth since 1979, according to new research from the U.S. Department of Education, Labor, and Work Force Census Bureau. Unemployed jobs lost 4.81 percent in 2016, indicating a year-over-year decline of 43.9 percent. Incomes for unemployment increased by 2.59 percent in December, composing the largest annual income growth since 1979. Unemployed jobs among Americans represent the largest growth in the job market in decades, according to CDI. Cruelty jobs increased by 3.81 percent in the second quarter after reaching 43,000 in December in 2015. Unemployment increased at a yearly rate of 69 percent in the first quarter of 2016, reaching a season-long low of 13.5 percent. The latest data come from a survey of Americans’ attitudes toward full-time working between 1890 and 2015. Trends toward healthcare, teachers, government and social sector workers, and other work forces represent the largest growth in the self-work force in decades. New unemployed lost 30.1 percent in the first quarter, making this the largest single-week quarter recorded between 1890 and 2015, according to CDI. The attitude toward full-time working declined from 22 percent in December in 2015 to 72 percent in 2016. Rates of unemployment, on the other hand, grew in the first quarter, expanding from 71 percent in December in 2015 to a season-long low of 13.5 percent in 2016 (see chart). On the other hand, unemployment grew at the fastest rate since 1979, according to CDI. Incomes for full-time earners declined from 71 percent in December in 2015 to 72 percent in December in 2016. Trends for full-time earners toward healthcare, teachers, government and social sector workers grew by 2.59 percent, rising 15 percent from Dec. 8, 2016 to reach a season-long low of 13.5 percent. Incomes for employment lost 37.8 percent in December in 2016, reflecting a year-over-year decline of 60.7 percent. Incomes for newly unemployed and newly unemployed fell by 3.81 percent in 2016, compared with 56.8 percent in 2016, according to new data from the U.S. National Bureau of Labor Statistics. Cruelty net income increased from $11.34 trillion in the second quarter to $11.2 trillion, according to financial research firm Barclays Capital Management. Net income, which is adjusted for corporate tax totals, increased from $2 trillion in the second quarter of 2016 to $11.2 trillion in 2016. The increase is driven by several factors, including the debt crisis, ongoing economic deflation and widespread inequality in job markets, including social dis integration, rising uncertainty among working-age Americans toward the middle class, ongoing monetary growth, shifts in U.S. government policy and the start of the consumer price bubble, according to CDI. Unemployed jobs lost 4.81 percent representing a year-over-year decline of 44.9 percent. Trends for unemployment increased from 13.5 percent in December in 2015 to 13.5 percent in the first quarter of 2016, representing 18 percent growth in 2016 versus a 12 percent growth in December in 2015. This story was originally updated on Dec. 8, 2016. Dallas Police said there were no arrests after the deadly shooting of a Border Patrol officer at an apartment module in the George Campbell neighborhood. According to Lt. Col. Edward C. Hicks Jr., the officer, the top serant on the U.S. Marshals Service, Border Patrol and Ambulance Service teams, was walking out of the parking lot of his apartment module when he suffered a bullet wound to the head. He was taken to the hospital. Dallas Mayor Michael Bloomberg said the shooting was an \"inflammatory attack on the city's law-enforcement capabilities.\" \"This is a totally senseless act,\" Dallas Police spokeswoman Jeanne Shumsky said in a statement. \"While this deadly incident is one of the city's most heartbreaking events overall, the Border Patrol officer's family will extend their sympathies to the victims and extend deepest sympathies to all those left uncathed by this incident. We will update our thoughts soon.\" It was the fifth deadly shooting in Dallas by police in modern department history, and sixth in 40 years, according to the National Bureau of Investigation Statistics. It is the largest among 36 shootings in police history, and the largest among 74 fatal shootings dating back to 1979."
      },
      {
        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000872",
        "text": "Bully, who was near Independence Avenue on the south side of Seventh Avenue Friday afternoon, was shot and killed in the back of his car. Bully went home with a neighbor until 2:30 p.m. Friday afternoon, where he was shot multiple times in an armour, police said. Bully was in attendance for an autopsy, authorities said. Police said the victim reported he’d been sitting in a separate vehicle from the burglar when officers went to the scene because he had a hidden gun. Authorities in the alleged victim reported he was being held under the burglar’s gun and drugs, the Cookington County District Attorney’s Office said during an internal news conference Friday afternoon. It was not disclosed when the nature of the crime was committed or which drugs were taken. Authorities have not released a final description as of this time. Police have been unable to establish the victim’s identity. J.W. McCarthy Galliday, M.Ka. Bully, is a resident of a property near Independence Avenue and arrested in connection with burglars, according to the Cookington County District Attorney’s Office. Bully has had a history of encounters with burglars, including charges for firearm possession, the Cookington County Attorney’s Office said. Bully is facing one charge of aggravated attempted robbery, four aggravated robberies and three counts of criminal possession, authorities said, and he is being held in the Cookington County Seventh Jail on an $82,000 cash bond. Those found guilty can carry a sentence of up to 10 years. FRANTO BICK, Ohio — Workers have been carrying oil and gas down the Cleveland River for a long time. When the oil ended up in two wellhouses along the bank in northwest Ohio in March 2009, the U.S. Coast Guard saved the hydrochemical excavation fire that ravaged both wells, leaving more than 1 million acres inundated with oil and gas. The gas swept into a defaced building immediately next door to an excavation facility, and leaked deeper into the 2,200-square-foot Cleveland River wellhouse, slicing plume of concrete across its roof and smashing it off the river. The agency said it plans to have the wellhouses repaired by early Saturday. President Barack Obama has ordered the full repair of the wellhouses, shipyards and wellhouses to be completed by early Saturday, along with the surrounding infrastructure. An Interior Department spokesman announced that about 5,500 employees from the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service were expected at a press conference in Cincinnati on Saturday afternoon to present their timeline for the repair of the wellhouses. About a week after the wellhouse’s collapse, U.S. Rep. Carl Rove announced that the federal government needs up to $9 billion to complete infrastructure repairs after the destruction of five buildings. The Interior Department will present the repair details over the next few days, though no specific timetable for the operation has been set. Adam Kawok, a spokesperson for the Oil Exports Corporation, which operates the wellhouses, said that the disaster was “the most massive oil export disaster we have seen in the past decade.” That comment raised questions about the design of the wellhouses, the availability of the hydrochemical excavation facility, as well as the cost of the operation. Patrick Monter, district chairman of the Cleveland County Board of Directors, which managed the wellhouses, said he estimates the repair will cost about $2 billion compared to an existing $1.8 billion operation that Cleveland has underway now along the bank, though he said it may take much longer. “You don’t know how long it takes,” Monter said of the repair. The full repair of more than four dozen wellhouses was completed Saturday afternoon at the Cleveland Hotel along the Cleveland River by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, which managed most of the wellhouses, the Ohio state Department of Natural Resources said in a statement. The Cleveland River wellhouse was refurbished Saturday afternoon. (David Olsen/The Washington Post) Cleveland County Coast Guard Sgt. James Denis said that “the stakes and the coin isn’t going to stop there.” He said that there will be more and more after the disaster is over. “Any time we allow something like this to happen with a major spill, you have to wonder how are we going to be able to deal with that?” Denis said. “It’s going to take about 10 years to fix the wellhouse and remove probably about 1 million acres of concrete.” The Interior Department will continue work on a solution that will cost about $200 million to fix and replace a wellhouse that is located on the northwest bank of the Cleveland River, officials said."
      },
      {
        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000907",
        "text": "There were a lot of other things that I was really focused on about camera and that was the type of thing that you have to do in about 30 minutes on a typical day just to get better. But what really stood out was the way that you’re able to create, whether it’s in focus or on the other side of the screen. It’s actually an incredible experience. On a typical day you can pick up a photo box and you’ll be able to create a perfect image in about a minute. So what really stood out, that was incredible. It was a lot of trying to get better from day to day, and I think a lot of trial and error. So it was really amazing to be there. But one of the big things that really entried me out of working with Google was being able to do that once and for all. If moving to motion makes a big difference, then let me start by saying that moving to motion can make a big difference. That happened to me because I was actually moving pretty much the same thing, not with my feet and not with my eyes in the same way. It meant that if I’m working on video, and I can start and stop motion in the same way that I can start motion, then I have to figure it out every time I do it. Every time I did that I was changing how the facial level works. Now, I was like, “Oh, I have to figure it out. If I stop it, I'm in focus and it works a little bit differently, then I switch on the camera and when I close my eyes it works exactly the same way. And then then I have to do all that facial level in order to work and then I can figure it out how to interact with the facial level of that video.” So that was really one of the things that really benefited me, was that it added new facial tiers that were built into so that I could focus on that video. That’s one of the nice things about working at Google for so long was being able to make a lot of money using that. If I had a camera that had that facial level, I could go out and make a little money. So one of the nice things about working with Google is that it added those facial tiers, and one of the big things that really really aided me was being able to make a lot of money using that. On virtual reality specifically. One of the big things that I have learned recently is that the majority of the work that’s done at Google is virtual reality and there’s a certain percentage of features that are now freely available in the company. In terms of looking at augmented reality, I think a lot of us people are like, “Okay, maybe this will get better,” or we’re like, “Not really, not really.” But one of the things that really benefited out of working with Google was the new Hello AI that we’re going to be releasing for a first time later this year. You released Hello basically a little over a year ago, probably based on your experiences with Google. You discovered the new AI and I think it was such an awesome experience. Sergei Paranov kind of arose right into working on it, and Al Gore started to work on it pretty soon after it was released. What are some of the other features about the new AI that aided it? It’s one of those things that I discovered a couple years ago and is a big improvement on my stint, that I’m finally learning to do using the new AI. So it’s a really big improvement on my own initial discovery. I think when I work on an app the one thing I really can do is use anything I don’t like. As long as you’re mostly educated in that you really want to be able to use and use that AI. Hello is a hard app, hard to make. It’s just gonna be a really cute app, if you don’t have it. I think there’s a lot of aspects to it, as long as you’re sitting on a chair, you really want to be able to use and use it for animation. Not everyone should be using it at the moment. I think when I work on an app the one thing I really can do is use anything I don’t like. As long as you’re educated in that, there’s nothing wrong with animation. You really want to be able to do with that AI. As long as you’re mostly educated in it, you really want to be able to use and use it for animation. Drive wheel for wheel across a street like a bus. More on Hello."
      },
      {
        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000910",
        "text": "Lincoln began the offseason with St. Louis Cardinals and spent the previous two seasons as a deputy offensive coordinator with the St. Louis Rams. Lincoln was named the team’s deputy offensive coordinator in the middle of the season and returned him to his team after every game to make sure he continues to have a significant impact on the team. Until after the 2017 season, the Chargers signed Lincoln to an automatic one year contract to play for the rest of the season. 25) Aaron Green, President of NFL Operations Perhaps the biggest praise of Aaron Green is his ability to match up with size, athleticism and creativity during his first two seasons. Green is one of the most experienced QBs in all of football and weighs only 6’2′′ 23.8 pounds. He has the size, strength, and speed to stand out as a valuable addition to any incoming offensive tackle on the roster. There is a little doubt whether Green’s size and speed will have a significant impact next season, but his injuries are a reason for him to stay strong in the NFL. Despite being hurt or undersized during each of his three seasons with the Lions, Aaron Green has demonstrated to need significant improvements if he is to emerge as a dominant player in 2017. 24) Stephen Pannon, President of Football Operations Stephen Pannon has built a spectacular coaching career with his team. After his stint as the deputy offensive coordinator with the Buffalo Bills, he then began a five-year stint as vice president of football operations and as vice president of special teams for the Green Packers. He served on active duty as the NFL’s coach during his first two seasons on the team. Pannon proved himself a willing special team and someone willing to take advantage of the ball when his need arises. For now, however, Pannon appears to be willing to let through on his opportunity whilst enjoying a bad start to the 2017 campaign. MONTLELAND, Minnesota - Sen. Jessica DeCaprio and her colleague, Kelly Anne DeCaprio, say President Trump's executive order to deporte undocumented native illegal immigrants in connection with last week's terrorist attack on St. John Medical Center in Minnesota needs to go before a Congress vote. \"For the first time in weeks, President Trump wants to put up a vote on the deportation of native-born terrorist immigrants and it's a way for the president to contribute to his agenda through a bill that would finally speak the message to the U.S. people,\" DeCaprio said. \"We've seen so many acts of mass violence in this country and it's just too late for that Congress of the United States act on the detention and deportation of terrorist immigrants.\" DeCaprio and her colleague are championing a bill that would allow the courts to intervene on the executive order and a bill that would require the government to issue an immigration status certificate for all Mexicans and non-Mexico, as well as card holders. \"We finally have a police in this country and a court,\" DeCaprio said. \"My people respect the court, and I respect the court, but it's very different between people who respect the court and people who respect the court.\" Sen. Jason Buck, an Orlando Republican, who served on the Senate's Homeland Security Committee during the Orlando Springs terrorist attacks, said there was \"no legitimate reason\" for his colleague to back the executive order. \"It's not a political question. It's a bi-partisan question of whether the federal government should issuing him a medical card instead of issuing him a work permit,\" Sen. Matt Jones, an Orlando Republican said. \"It's just unrealistic that the U.S. Senate would be required to vote on this bill.\" Critics say Trump's executive order to deporte a half million undocumented terrorist immigrants in February was too partisan and needed to go to a vote. Only one Senate Democrats voted to back the executive order in the Senate's closing session on Tuesday, two days before Trump celebrates his victory. Republicans controlled the Senate overwhelming to give the Trump administration the opportunity to stand a vote on the bill, but there is no deadline for the bill to be approved before March 3. The Feb. 1 terrorist attack on St. John Medical Center in Minnesota was the most deadly attack on record in the state. A whopping 26 percent of Minnesota Republicans voted to back the executive order, compared to 12 percent of Democrats and 22 percent opposed. An NBC News/Ipsos/ORC poll conducted in the U.S. Senate on Thursday found that 39 percent of U.S. voters supported deporting terrorist immigrants, versus 44 percent opposed."
      },
      {
        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000950",
        "text": "The main reason for this is the rise andaging of the PlayStation and Vita. And while the PlayStation and Vita have soared and aged gradually, they are doing so in a next-generation way that doesn’t require the same updates as their earlier predecessors. And while the PlayStation is dying at a much faster rate, it is still able to do it on its own. The other major difference between these two platforms is that the Vita isaging at a nearly identical rate, while the Vita — the next power generation — progress along a different path. WASHINGTON (Reuters) President Donald Trump on Monday unveiled a deal that would weaken U.S. economic sanctions and halt U.S. cyber defense measures aimed at North Korea. FILE PHOTO Reuters shows U.S. President Donald Trump meeting with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un, as North Korean leader Kim Jong Un walks near a ballistic missile defense system in Pyongyang, March 7, 2017. REUTERS/Eric Whitsville The trade deal marks a major shift in Trump’s tactics at a time when Washington is preparing to weaken U.S. sanctions against North Korea. Sources close to the deal said it could lead to further reconciliations, including a halt of U.S. military exercises in North Korea and a reduction of U.S. sanctions. “The United States’s decision to advance a wide-range ballistic missile defense system against North Korea is a major win for allies, who must comply with U.S. and international law and procedure,” the U.S. National Security Council said in a statement. “This is a major shift in allies’ strategic efforts to counter growing U.S.-made terrorist threats.” Trump’s pan-Pacific deal push marks the end of a long trend of reducing U.S. sanctions against North Korea, an issue he continues to push forward over concerns it would lead to a full-scale war. In February, South Korean President Donald Trump issued a statement saying he would halt some U.S. military exercises aimed at North Korea that now include ballistic missile defense systems and intercontinental missile combat. But he has made clear his administration will take the course. About eight months after winning reelection, the Senate approved a comprehensive defense spending bill intended at addressing the threat that the country is hurtling toward another nuclear war. The House approved wide-ranging defense spending cuts in July 2017, leading the Senate to set a $264 billion budget for the 2018 fiscal year, which Congress is expected to approve next year. Trump’s administration has vowed not to accept the cuts, citing concerns that they would fall too weak. The trade deal, led by Trump’s national security adviser, General Michael Flynn, former US Army Secretary of State James Mattis and South Korean President Yoki Hawabana, is a major shift in U.S. national security strategy aimed at preparing for a full-scale war by weakening defense systems and reducing U.S. sanctions on trade. North Korean leader Kim Jong Un, who opened talks with US President Donald Trump on Monday, is slated to be at the forefront of the two-day Asia Pacific summit in Seoul. The deal bolsters the Trump administration’s long-standing insistence that U.S. sanctions have fallen weak on North Korea. The U.S.-North Korean summit is Trump’s first trip since coming into office in January, while Trump will travel to North Korea to demand a halt and abandon all U.S. cyber defense measures. Sources familiar with the trade deal said the move marks the end of a pan-Pacific trend aimed at weakening U.S. cyber defense measures. North Korea would also benefit from the deal, along with Japan, South Korea and China in signing a zero-proliferation pact. The two countries will likely reach agreement on a new nuclear pact in 2018. The United Nations has released a video showing a Palestinian woman being kidnapped by a man in the Waf Barim neighborhood of eastern Jerusalem by the Israeli security forces. The UN has pledged to improve its position in the al-Qqaida region and help identify the culprits of the attack. The new pledge comes at a time of tension over certain areas, including the possibility of depletion in oil and lowering gasoline prices. The U.N.-owned news agency has released a video showing a Palestinian woman being kidnapped by a man in the Waf Barim neighborhood of the al-Qqaida region of Jerusalem by Israeli security forces in illegally occupied Gaza on June 9.."
      },
      {
        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000973",
        "text": "House majority Leader Paul Ryan said he doesn't believe the Republican tax plan will reduce the federal budget deficit — and has been unaware of any increase in new tax cuts. (The Washington Post) In a plan he presented at the Congressional Budget Office last month, the President announced plans to reduce the corporate tax rate to a minimum wage to 25 percent. According to the White House, the Republican plan would include a $74 trillion increase — 34.2 percent as opposed to 9.6 percent — and an additional $1 trillion in new tax cuts. Trump on Thursday unveiled a Republican revision to Obamacare’s Affordable Care Act plan, which specifically calls for $74 trillion over the next decade. “Throughout the course of this action, I’m going to address the need to reduce the federal budget deficit by an important measure,” he told the Congressional Budget Office. That figure would include a $17 trillion increase in the corporate income taxes, as opposed to a Democratic plan that supports slashing the Affordable Care Act, raising the minimum wage to 2.2 percent, and lowering the corporate tax rate by 25 percent. The House Judiciary Chairman said the value of the cost of raising the corporate tax rate would be about exactly the same as a Democratic plan, but said he understands the Republican plan would increase the corporate tax rate for low-income individuals by about $5 trillion. “I don’t think that’s a lot,” Rep. David Rose (R-N.H.) told The Washington Post, “but it’s going to be. Not a lot.” The cost of raising the corporate tax rate for the rich could be between 9.2 percent and 50 percent, much of which will result from tax cuts that President Obama announced last month. When he leaves office, Rose said, he would raise the coverage rate for individuals without insurance by between 2.2 percent and 1.5 percent. The Republican plan would reduce that amount of federal deficit to $191.2 trillion as opposed to 8.0 percent, Rose said. The White House on Thursday unveiled a Republican revision to the Affordable Care Act, which calls for increasing the minimum wage rate for half or more of the rich, and lowering the coverage rate for individuals aged 50 and over without insurance. That plan would reduce the corporate tax rate to 16.3 percent as opposed to 1.5 percent. Similar to a Democratic plan, the federal budget deficit would amount to $74 trillion, less than the amount it would take to reduce the federal budget deficit by 25 percent, officials said. The plan specifically calls for an additional $74 trillion over the next four years. On Thursday, the President said he would continue to deal with the deficit, including lowering the corporate tax rate to 9.3 percent as opposed to 1.5 percent, along with a 2.2 percent increase for low-income individuals without insurance. (White House) The Republican plan would reduce the federal budget deficit by about $5 trillion over the next decade, but it would reduce the federal deficit by about $14 trillion. Trump said Thursday that the White House would continue to deal with the deficit, although he has been unaware of the plan. On Thursday, Trump said he supports slashing the corporate tax rate by 25 percent, as opposed to a Democratic plan, but the House Democrat majority leader said there’s “no way forward” to the idea of slashing the rates. Representative Jim Issa (R-Utah), the ranking member of the House Financial Services Committee, said he was unaware of the Republican plan, although the House Judiciary Chairman would be unaware of Trump’s plan. “I think there’s no question of the economic impact, and it’s up to the Judiciary to go through the details,” House Financial Services Committee Vice Chairman Bob Busch said in a statement. “It’s impossible to overstate the economic impact of any of these figures, and it’s up to the House Judiciary to go through the details.” majority Leader Paul Ryan (R-Texas) said he doesn’t believe the Republican plan will reduce the federal budget deficit by several important measures, including a reduction in corporate income taxes and an additional trillion in new tax cuts. He said he understands the Republican plan would address the need to reduce the federal budget deficit. Ryan said the Republican plan would reduce the budget deficit by about $14 trillion over the next decade, but his office did not verify whether it would indeed reduce the budget deficit. The White House on Thursday unveiled another Republican revision to the Affordable Care Act, which calls for increasing the minimum wage rate for half or more of the rich, and lowering the coverage rate for individuals age 50 and over. Among the Trump proposals presented before the Congressional Budget Office, included $17 trillion in cuts to the Congressional Health Care Act. (AP)"
      },
      {
        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_128_nfe-000986",
        "text": "A Techall single set up Baltimore's first hat trick and tied the match in the 58th minute. Anaheim trailed by one straight point in the 76th minute, though the Padres were silent in the 81st minute. Araka Sansaino made his first start to the regular season by bouncing left-handed ball past Orioles goalkeeper Robbie Enron, keeping their lead at 4-3. But in the 83th minute, Matt Gates punted a two-run homer, ending the longest regular scoring streak any MLB manager has ever gone without a point. He ended the match with four singles in nine attempts. That was the Padres' first win in the regular season. \"I pitched really well,\" Sansaino said. \"I pitched really well off the bat and hit my hip into the air. That was the best of the season. There were some bad runs and some good shots, but we had a good start to the season. We are playing well.\" Still marred by knee cancer Sansaino wasn't expected to make an MLB debut, but he was hospitalized later that week after surgery showed he slit left side of the knee, according to The Des Moines Register. In the 86th minute, Sansaino pulled off a home run against Tampa Bay, who went into the second half with a 7-8 deficit. Matt Gates was scratched on his home run when a straight faced ball bit into the air near his upper-middle pitch. \"I had a ball going over my own pitch. I was extremely lucky,\" Sansaino said. \"All I am going to say is that it was a long home run, and I was chasing the ball on both sides of the pitch and I hit a hip into the air. I may have been able to escape the ball because I pitched the ball well.\" SQUIRNING ABOUT THE HUMS The Padres took the win, met with an overwhelming enthusiasm from the fans, as the crowd ringed with banners flinging for a win and a chance to become their next MLB manager. But it was Sansaino's actions that prevented him from being afforded a gold medal, when he punted another homer with just 12:26 left. \"Nothing changes after this,\" Sansaino said. \"For me, it was a big defeat and I am happy about it. It was a great game in a lot of ways, but it was a tough defeat and a big win that had no impact on my career. More than perfectly, it happened.\" Correction: This story first appeared earlier this year when Padres fans were a part of the Orioles in 2012. Verizon Wireless Co. (TC.O) will not enforce President Trump's travel ban for mentally ill Americans, a claim the U.S. Federal Trade Commission has denied. Verizon Wireless passengers said the company ultimately would not have to send their blood to a doctor to determine if they were mentally ill -- meaning those clients had no choice. Verizon Wireless users who filed the lawsuit with the Federal Trade and Exchange Commission, said in a Tuesday filing in the Ninth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals that they will not have to send their blood to a doctor to determine if they are mentally ill. The plaintiff is Eric Fax, an inspector general for the Department of Transportation, the U.S. Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, and a commissioner of the Department of Health and Human Services. The ACCC, which has closely monitored the Trump administration's interpretation of the ban, is in favor of the new rules. \"We have grave concerns about President Trump's proposed travel ban for mentally ill Americans, and his interpretation of the GOP Affordable Care Act,\" Fax said in a statement to The Huffington Post. \"If the Trump administration doesn't stand behind its travel ban, it doesn't appear likely that it will.\" Omar Andher, a former AT&T executive and co-founder of Midway Investments Inc., a leading mentally ill real estate developer that filed the lawsuit, said it isn't enough to slow his business down. \"I have clients who are working in businesses all over the country, so they cannot get their blood sent out by the doctor we're talking about,\" Andher said. \"They are not getting an affordable medical care in this country.\" LPIM, a group advocating for passing the Affordable Care Act, wouldn't comment on the president-elect's decision, a spokesman for LPIM told CNN."
      }
