        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-000551",
        "text": "Under the terms of the New Plans Act, licenseholders would receive an average of $800 annually for American development of new plans. That money will go toward paying tax breaks for individual plans, and if the bill passes the House, about 25 percent of the funding could expire. 7. Title V The Affordable Care Act would cut funding to about a million insured children before they turn 30. Title V would end Medicare's funding after another two years, leaving many of those children eligible for a lifetime's treatment. But Title V would also make health care easier and cheaper for older children with high-deficit health conditions, who would be eligible to decline treatment until the age of two years. It would also make health care cheaper for insured children whose health conditions are less severe, Gerkovich said. 8. Obamacare bill for repeal in the House? Republicans like Rep. Dayton Cantor of Florida, R-Ala., said repeal of the bill could come with a simple vote, and that a majority of Americans would vote no. Cantor urged them not to send the bill back to the House. \"Voining Obamacare is a big step toward saving millions from health insurance,\" he said. 9. Costs for drug replacement? Republicans like Rep. Rick Santorum, R-N.Y., who wrote the Affordable Care Act, say Republicans shouldn't be in bad spirits to leave the country with a more expensive drug replacement than an earlier version of repealing Obamacare, which they say is projected to expir in 2022. Overall, Santorum said, compared to removing the earlier version, this saves between $1 billion and $1.6 billion in taxpayer dollars — about $12 billion in savings compared to the version that Republicans are now trying to abolishe. Matt Wade is a fellow at the Matt Wade Center on Public Health Policy at Western Washington University in Denver, Colo., Follow him on Twitter @MaxRavenMD. With recent reports that the New York Giants were trading Tom Jackson to the Houston Texans for an offensive-heavyness tight end, it’s pretty clear that the team wasn’t in good spirits to make the right hit on Jackson. The Giants did ensure a trade that sent the fourth-round, $82 million draft pick Paul Adams for a $1 million undrafted free agent took place five days to the start of the season, but that could have led to a different outcome. Jackson, the fifth-ranked pass catcher of the 2014/2015 NFL draft, has 34.5 sacks over his last two seasons on the job for the Steelers and a few fewer passes to running backs for touchdowns. He ran for 2,250 receiving yards and 80 touchdowns in just 51 games last season. So now that the Giants are handling tight end Joey Bradley, rumors are beginning to circle that general manager Bill Brady is re-acquiring the Houston-born receiver and pass catcher Tim Steeler to work alongside Jackson. That speculation is mainly getting around as bad as it’s getting, especially among the Giants, who are already cashing in on Jackson ahead of the fourth consecutive NFL draft. But during his appearance on Chiefs radio on Wednesday, Jackson didn’t say whether he’d be bringing back Texans tight end Adams, who has been on the Steelers’ books for three seasons as an undrafted free agent. The 37-year-old Steeler said Wednesday that he could add Jackson to that mix next season. “I definitely know him and he has a lot of faith in me,” Jackson said. “He’s one of the best NFL players I’ve ever met so I’m so grateful for him. He’s such a fun guy to coach and he’s a great athlete so he likes to work with. So I think they’ll do everything in their power to sign Tom. “The tight end pair was the best for me in the third, fourth and fifth rounds. They have a lot of great potential and he’s a fun guy to work with. They helped get me to the NFL next level.” Jackson sent Adams to his first NFL game as a designated tight end in January, where he started four games and rushed for 12 touchdowns. First he said he planned to try and play football alongside his teammates next season. If he does that, he’ll want to stay with his family. Jackson and Adams began playing together as a tight end in 2009, but failed to start each other for six games after being placed under suspension in a first-round loss to a quarterback in Atlanta. Adams only played in the first 15 games and did not play until he was signed to Carolina one more spring in 2014."
