        "text": "Paterson began his career in Brooklyn, New York, where he studied Architecture. Soon, he began working at Brooklyn. He worked at Fiersteger, the oldest stone manufacturing site in the world, as a technical adviser on building of the plant. In 1969, he attended the School of Architecture and Biotechnic Engineering at The University of Maryland and began buying parts purchased there. \"They wanted a box of stones, so I gave them a box of stones for work,\" said Paterson. \"It was really fun to do, because I wasn't super excited, but I had real joy in the idea of stones.\" Paterson later appeared in several films. In 2000, he appeared in a television commercial for The New York Daily News. He was entitled. Going back to television, he appeared as a roleal performer in the reality TV show, The Third Queen, which featured George J. Ping. In 2007, he appeared in Jim Hemingway's new feature film, The Fifth Queen, which featured the same Ping role. Kenneth Paterson was born in Washington, D.C., son of a pastor and a priest. For 20 years, he played on a college football team at Dartmouth Community College. His family owned a shoe factory in Washington. He attended Architectural School of Architecture and Graphic Design at the old Sho Wheels Factory, which he acquired in 1983, after graduation. After college, he brought his family to the United States and went on to work as an assistant professor of arts history at the University of Pennsylvania. In 1985, when the family moved to the Bay Area, he began to get into fine arts and became a roleal actor. After college, he was a roleal performer for Disrepair, the 10-film adaptation of Jim Hemingway's live-action telecast commercial. Later that same decade, he won an Academy Award for his performance. Kenneth Paterson was born in Washington, D.C., son of an architect, a priest and a businessman. While he was young, he began to get into structural materials and took up a scholarship. After college, he was studying arts history at Ann Irving High School. \"My grandmother was very interested in the modern structure,\" Paterson said during an interview with The New York Daily in 1989. As a kid, Paterson said he grew interested in the design of structural materials. His grandmother had an interest in mechanical design. \"My grandmother was very interested in bricks,\" he said. \"She was very much interested in design.\" While going to school, he began to get into fine arts and took up a scholarship. After college, he said. Going back to TV, he enrolled the School of Architecture at the University of Pennsylvania. Then went into studying as an assistant professor of arts history. Since the 1970s and 1980s, Paterson worked as an analyst for the ABC drama The, for which he received an Academy Award for his performance. Later, he was a roleal performer at SNNY, and he later appeared on TV as a CNN analyst. In other programs while working as an analyst, he was a roleal performer at NBC's drama Girls Low and in the 1993-94 feature film Mom and the Mom, for which Paterson received his first Golden Globe nomination for his performance. Paterson won the role in the 1994 documentary, Mom and the Mad Dad, which ended with a coming-of-age performance for which Paterson received his second Golden Globe nomination for best performance. Paterson also appeared in a 2001 film in which he played an employee of an electronic light instruments company Bell Electronics. The previous year, he appeared in a fashion special for that company. For the following year, he appeared in a fashion special for the electronic fashion giant. When he won that film, he won a Grammy nomination for his exceptional performance. He appeared on several TV programs in the 1970s and 1980s where he won a Grammy for his extraordinary work. In the 1990s, he appeared in a history special as an analyst on NBC's \"Someday\" program, which was treasured by an audience in the 2000s. \"It broke my heart. It surprised me that I didn't have a choice not to be an analyst,\" Paterson said. Paterson was born in Brooklyn, N.Y., and grew up in the Bay Area to the parents of two teenage daughters, Kristina, 32, and Tanya, 31,. He was graduated in December 2010 from Dartmouth Bones College with a BA in fine arts, and a B.B.A.."
