        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-000878",
        "text": "There would be a \"clonking period\" of tax reform by the end of Donald Trump's first term, saying that it wipes all the support markets have had for an economic recovery in the 21st century, and ruins the OECD forecast that it would reduce corporate spending by 9.5 per cent over the next century. The comments came in an interview Friday during the primary day of the Republican Presidential Caucuses, which has Trump's lead at 83 per cent. On a conference call on the Saturday before the June 7 Caucuses, Trump told advisers it would take until mid-2020 to agree on a deal to cut Social Security benefits, helping by repealing Obamacare. \"I don't think this is the deal that ever got made,\" he said. \"It's a deal that really didn't get made.\" Presidential contender Charles B. Coddings promises economies grow US GDP by half, mitigating resistance to tax cuts As a candidate for the presidency, Trump is not going to pledge comprehensive tax reform, and he is not saying that it will create more jobs. He simply plans to reduce the unemployment rate from the current level of 14 per cent. He will soon announce two programs that he said would take the next step in reducing tax burden. He would push for a narrow budget gap, which some economists see as the failure of government efforts to cope with last year's austerity crisis. Another popular goal is to cut retail taxes for the rich, and campaigners concerned he could cut it by 4.5 per cent as food prices rise. In order to restore the support of Democratic and Republican elites across the nation, he would increase the annual income of the wealthiest American by 3.6 per cent, up from 6.2 per cent in 2010, when the official poverty rate stood at 15 per cent. Trump announced that he would write off the increase, saying it would help finance a financial recovery, but some economists worry that he will pledge to raise corporate revenues would be an unfair signal to his campaign. Scott Davis, a professor of economics at the University of British Columbia who called the long-term proposal a lightning attack, said that it would set a dangerous precedent for an economy whose growth was undermatched in the 1970s, and that tax cutting attitudes re-surfaced after the Great Depression. For many industries, income taxes have been skyrocketing due to the lack of jobs. \"Workers are more well-off than the rich, so there aren't jobs,\" he said. More broadly, Davis said that beginning next year, markets should seek a 10 per cent reduction in taxes on consumer goods. That would reduce the cost of goods that are most expensive to produce, such as manufacturing or packaging. Reducing the tax burden would ultimately mean more jobs in the economy, he said. That is where Trump would put what analysts call a \"cloking period\" — the time that businesses benefitt from massive tax cuts — at play, he said. A tax cut would push trade unions and capitalist parties toward a zero-GDP scenario at the expense of the poor. \"It would shift the policy narrative in favor of businesses at a time when unemployment is rising even higher,\" he said. Americans are already one of just 13 percent to experience a back-to-back economic recession, according to a New Economic Policy Research Institute poll. As such, the tax cut is a turning point for what's next — a dramatic overhaul of the law. The tax cut has been a roller coaster, but conservative politicians and economists have been campaigning to get a better deal. Trump is not the only proponent of tax reform to receive ovation from the White House, which Anton Friedman Flynn of the Center for a New America used in January to conclude that the tax reform would benefit only the rich. \"It's difficult for people to look at this slope and realize just how bad it has been over the past 50 to 100 years,\" said Tim Piper, a fiscal analyst at the University of California, Berkeley. \"It's a difficult slope to look at,\" Davis said. \"It's the best projection we could have in a Donald Trump administration on how to narrow the poverty gap and reduce corporate spending.\" 'What's ahead:' Once Trump takes office, the tax cut will run for another half of the century until next year. But as his campaign has been claiming over the past months, Trump has not reneged on all campaign promises. He said in January it would save taxpayers around $800 billion, a carbon tax would reduce the nation's budget deficits, and earlier this year he promised to end the retail tax on consumer goods."
