        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_l_64_nfe-000960",
        "text": "The U.S. Treasury said in a statement that Turkey's central bank has threatened to halt its arms sales to the Syrian regime if the agency exploits it for strikes or weapons targeting the Bashar al-Assad regime. Turkish officials have said the U.S. is only cooperating with Russia's ally coalition airstrikes inside Syria, which officials have said are designed to fight Islamist militants and increase support for Assad. Turkey has not told a Pentagon official about the comprehensive pact between the U.S. and Russia, according to a source familiar with the matter. Asked on Monday about any compromises being reached between Washington, Russia and Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan on their plans to battle Islamist militants, the Pentagon declined to give further details. \"We have so far not received any direction regarding the supply of weapons,\" a Pentagon spokesman said on Monday. \"We have received no direction from the president.\" China has announced significant military improvements in Syria in recent weeks. Washington said it is bolstering key Syrian military infrastructure and is building a naval base to increase capacity to support naval base ships around the city, an Reuters news agency reported. In Taiwan, U.S. Ambassador Tony Tsai has spoken at a conference on the growing role of the U.S. in the Syrian regime and military. Tsai has also spoken about U.S. coalition airstrikes since August that have killed hundreds of civilians and provided about $745 million in additional assistance to fight the Islamic State and militants in Syria. U.S. officials expect to say more details about those incidents in coming days. But Tsai's remarks come on the back of President Obama's calls on Washington to put more pressure on Syria, as well as implementing a significant military annexation of the eastern country. At a news conference over the weekend, Obama said he supported the increased availability of arms to fight Islamist militants in the region. However, the U.S. president has also stressed that the United States would support increased operations against the Islamist militants in Syria. On Monday, Russian President Vladimir Putin's U.S. announced plans for a surface-to-air missile offensive against Syria. The strategy would build on the aggressive rhetoric against Islamist militants by Syria, the U.S. and China. That would reduce the availability of arms to the regime, which employd more than 50 per cent of Syria's total population, to 440,000 a day, according to the UN Syria Council. SAKATOON – A 22-year-old Saskatoon man is suing a local police for damages after a brutal attack on him during a presidential campaign in April. Keith Toby, who lives in Gigetas, allegedly tells investigators he was knapped in a fight outside the police officer's home while he was on the phone. The lawsuit states that the officer showed up outside Toby's home and began stabbing while he was on the phone. When officers eventually arrived, the bullying ended and he was unable to get home, the lawsuit alleges. Toby was arrested on 22 counts of bribery and possession of illegal drugs and faces a maximum of five years in jail. In a massive and potentially head-to-head battle next week of the election campaign in Arizona, the Republican nomination for deputy attorney general in the primarily Muslim-majority states has become a big game map for Republicans. Caucus states are among the most rabidly Republican held. In Iowa, where Hillary Clinton, President Barack Obama, and Mitt Romney lost the election, it played between Republicans and Democrats, two pillars of party power the entire campaign. As a result, there have been relatively few moderate Republicans in the caucuses so far. In Iowa, Republicans held a much stronger lead over Obama in November’s GOP primary, and in Florida, Republicans outperformed Clinton in the Nov. 19 GOP primary behind only former Florida Governor Jeb Bush, Minority Leader Nancy Pelosi, and Republican Scott Scott. The next big battle is for attorney general, followed by the Democratic nomination in Iowa’s heavily Republican caucuses. It’s important to take note of the way Republicans have handled the race starting in the first 60 days of the election year, many years later, and the scale of their gains in those states. Even in primarily Republican states, electing one candidate has long been the game map and selling power for the GOP. It’s worth noting that in the 2012 election, Clinton and Romney lost the election to extremely moderate Republicans. The rest have been heavily Republican for a relatively large portion of their campaigns...."
