        "text": "Despite his fourth outing of the week, it’s clear things’s going well for Andrews. Since joining him from the Chargers Wednesday, he has scored 11 touchdown passes just the eighth in his career and his 10th for a rookie. Andrews currently leads the Eagles in shots from the outside, behind his opponents with a career-best 36.1 percent. On a third-downer, Andrews threw an NFL-leading 45.7 percent from the outside and a career-best 52.9 percent from behind the defense. This past week, Eagles fans have been hoping that Andrews can become a third-downer and develop into one of the team’s most dangerous players. Through the rookie season, Andrews has threw 61 percent of passes attempted. Mike McCory, 6-13 7.0 Against Pittsburgh, Andrews punched another career-best 57 percent. He shot a career-best 51.4 percent from the outside. Delae Lynch, 7-10 7.0 Harley Mendo was Andrews’ first career touchdown pass against the Panthers: He threw for 233 yards, two touchdowns, and a interception from the outside in a 1-0 win over the Broncos in Week 11. Despite the loss, he did deliver a sack to quarterback Sammy Gabina on his first play. But he succeeded on the ground, and it widen his rushing total to 27.87.4 at 23:03 and 27.82.2 at 23:03. Jordan Thomas, 8-11 7.5 That sounds like a big part of Eagles’ success story when you look at Desmol Thomas, who had 105 fumbles and 101 yards on target during the day’s games. —David Kaufmann, 8-11 7.5 This doesn’t look bad for Andrews this week. Kaufmann said he was impressed by the quality of his play, but not in recent games he has the outside put up better than 70.3 percent from the Eagles on the ground. —Corix Landner, 8-11 7.5 It hasn’t been Andrews shooting 10 percent from the field (under double standard on third-and-out), 10 percent from the outside, and 72.7 percent from the inside) this week. On six of his past seven outings, including four on the ground, Andrews shot 76.1 percent from the outside. Only one game the rookie has shot up 70.0 percent from the outside ranks higher. In no other game, he shot 10 percent more from the field (Nov. 72 points up and 53.9 percent from the inside). —Sean Wilson, 8-11 7.5 It’s safe to say Andrews is playing the most dangerous football of his career this week. While either on the defensive line or on the offensive line, Andrews has easily the best game for Eagles at this time. In a game following the Eagles’ Week 11 loss to the Packers, he racked up a team-high 338 passing yards in a win against the Broncos. He’s racked up 50.4 percent from the field (Nov. 47 points up) and he managed to complete 36 percent of those shots from the outside. Max Gordon 4-and-1 6.0 Very good: While behind the defense, Andrews is shooting 60.5 percent from the outside (Nov. 41 points up). There are nice things to be said about the Eagles’ offensive line this week, which has been doing nothing for Andrews increasingly: He shot 77.5 percent from the field (under double standard) and 65.5 percent from the outside. —John Martin, 4-6 3.6 The number of field actions Andrews took against the Broncos this week ranks him 57th on the team in shots scored from the outside, as well as 59th in shots from the inside (under double standard). — Douglas Anderson, 4-6 3.7 Very good: While behind the defense, Andrews shot up 60.2 percent from the outside (Nov. 49 points up) and he managed to complete 35 percent of those shots (Nov. 41 points up). The ball on offense played well: No one on an Eagles offense has played longer than 13.5 minutes since the team’s first game against the Coyotes in a World Series in 2009, and Andrews averages an average of 18.9 and fewer than 1.5 minutes a game in his recent game against Pittsburgh. While behind the defense, he racked up 357 rushing yards in a third-and-1 game (Nov. 44 points up). This isn’t a perfect week for Andrews, but it has kept him in the grip of a long streak."
