        "text": "Microplasmics is a global research company that uses simulation and machine learning to develop new technologies including nuclear reactors, hydropowers, and carbon dioxide capture technologies. Learn more from Microplasmics’ Web site: www.microplasmics.com The New Jersey board has approved plans that would stop a portion of the state's land being converted into a public monument. The New Jersey board of supervisors has approved a plan that would limit the amount of federal land in southern New Jersey by converting part of the state's Northern Desert, currently stranded, into a public property and historic monument. More than 6 million acres of federal land are now located in the area near Cook County, about 100 miles north of Newtown. The board of supervisors has approved plans that would limit the amount of federal land in the currently stranded area, and prevent that portion of federal land being converted into a public monument. The proposals are being considered by each judge on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit because they do not require a vote of a state legislature. They would limit the amount of federal land in the currently stranded area, and prevent that portion from being converted into a public monument. \"I'm disappointed by how radical this is,\" said New Jersey Pvt. David Dubpool, who serves on the state board of supervisors in Cook County. \"I think we've put our future in doubt, and I think it's a bad idea for the whole state.\" The New Jersey board of supervisors is scheduled to issue a final decree on Dec. 14 to stop local governments, state, county and provincial and federal planning agencies from reclaiming federal land in southern New Jersey. But Jared Richardson, spokesman for the state board of supervisors, said the problem is ongoing. \"There was a land shortage several years ago that completely affected the approval process,\" Richardson said. \"We need to get rules in place to root out this problem.\" Under state New Jersey law, the board of supervisors approve proposals directly before a legislative tools committee. A legislative tools committee can then submit proposals to the board of supervisors. The board passed a resolution last year requiring the entire board of supervisor to approve proposals. That vote drew bitter criticism from supervisors and in the planning industry. Earlier this year, the board of supervisors approved a resolution that would allow local governments to reclaim federal land in certain areas of southern New Jersey. When the board voted 42-94, the supervisorsvoted against that resolution that would allow a significant amount of federal land to return into a high-quality and historic monument. Jason Foisler, spokesman for the board of supervisors, said local governments have also passed legislation in the last year allowing the use of federal land in southern New Jersey. A federal land in Cook County was recently occupied and converted into a public monument. Foisler told the Denver Post that authorities are still in a critical position about their use of federal land. \"We're still in the process of figuring out what we can get local governments doing,\" he said. \"Plans are being developed for converting federal land into a high-quality and historic monument. We're still working on figuring out some options.\" Correction: An earlier version of this story was incorrect. Booktown has now been updated. Boston police announced the death of a 28-year-old man who stabed a man with a chain knife inside an apartment on Boston Avenue early Friday morning. The 24-year-old man was being treated for minor injuries and was taken to a Boston senior area hospital, police said. The Northeasterford County Sheriff's Office released a statement. 11:22 p.m. Boston police announced the death of a 28-year-old man who stabed a man with a chain knife inside an apartment on Boston Ave. It happened at a friend's dinner at Kennedy's Bar & Restaurant in the 4200 block of East Washington and Senior Street, Boston. Police say the gunman opened the door to the basement of the apartment and attacked the man. The 24--year-old man was treated for minor injuries and was taken off to a Boston area hospital. The man eventually attacked him, shouting, \"If you can't go, I'll let you go. I'm OK.\" Police didn't release the motive or say whether or not the gunman was involved in the attack. Police wouldn't say whether they have a motive. Tim Schultz, Northeasterford County Sheriff's Office, said the attack happened just before 10 am in the basement of the apartment block of East Washington Street. Two people were treated at the scene."
