        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-000216",
        "text": "Documents obtained by Amnesty International include videos of detaine sitting with a man in a cell as the detainee opens a locked door and violently wrestles the man. In one video, obtained by Reuters, the detainee raises his hand and beats the man, according to an Amnesty International reporter, who gave the report anonymous. This screengraked image shows a suspected terrorist tied to the Alpha-Carier car-side bombing on Wednesday, April 11, 2016, in Tifihar province in north-west Pakistan. (Ilayia/Reuters) NEW YORK (Reuters) - Pakistan authorities have detain a suspected terrorist tied to the Alpha-Carier car-side bombing earlier this month in south western Pakistan that killed two among three others, a public security official, asked not to speak on security matters, has said. Pakistan authorities detain a suspected terrorist tied to the Alpha-Carier bombing in April, one of the public security officials was quoted by a Washington Post reporter as saying. An official familiar with the situation, when asked, declined to speak for the record, but said security officials confirmed that the person was detained after the April 11 bombing. “Pakististan has a security policy. It is against terrorist attacks. It has a motivation behind the terrorist attacks,” said the official, who asked not to speak on confidential national security matters. Mohammad al Gharadhafi, a self-described follower of suspected terrorist attacker Mohammad Iblib, was detained by the bomb fighters on Aug. 19 2016. (Ilayia/Pajbah) Mohammad al Gharadhafi, detained in Tifihar, north-west Pakistan, presumed to be a follower of militant Mohammad Iblib, was initially bombed by Alpha-Carier vehicles and was later hijacked in the Aug. 19 attack that killed two and three other people. The U.S. military said in a statement that the deportation was a gesture to defend Pakistan against the anti-terrorist coalition that includes President Haider al-Qani. Mohammad al Gharadhafi, who was detained in April by Alpha-Carier fighters, mistaken him as a Sunni Muslim, now faces 20 years in detention, according to the U.S. military. Arrested in 2009, al Gharadhafi is scheduled to appear on June 25 at the Federal Criminal Court in Humphrey, Texas, to hear charges in connection with the attack. His defense lawyers ask that he be convicted by a military judge while he spend about 20 years in prison. On Thursday, Ali Hatterman, Pakistan’s foreign minister, said in his statement that the deportation is an indication of a shift towards fighting terrorism. “We have made this announcement both in the hands of a diplomat and in the hands of one senior public security official. I’m sure that the shift towards fighting terrorism is life and business as usual,” Hatterman said in a statement. Blood sugar levels have been rising in part thanks to walking, joking and street walks, as well as the survey of a blood sugar population of 1,000 young people aged 14 through 13. Young adults’ blood sugar levels have been rising over the past few years, according to a UCLA study that has tracked the rising trend for at least three years, during which researchers found young adults’ blood sugar levels were rising. The concept of “blood sugar bubble,” which refers to the 10 percent (0.1 to 0.1 percent) of a person’s total blood sugar level, has risen in the past few years. Recently, a number of studies have suggested that on-street activity would have a rising impact on young adults’ blood sugar levels. One study, published in December, which was funded by the National Academy of Sciences, suggested that on-street activity would have a rising impact on young adults’ blood sugar levels. That study looked at biodemurological data from surveys of 581,000 young adults in California from 2006 to 2014, and found that street walking significantly lowers blood sugar levels. It is one of several recent initiatives nationwide that is encouraging young adults to walk like chicks by lowering the street’s blood sugar levels. The study found no significant difference in blood sugar levels among young adults. “It’s still unclear if on-street activity will affect blood sugar levels,” said Dr. Megan Hulk, a biodemurologist at the UCLA School of Medicine who was not involved in the study. “We just don’t see an association between on-street activity and rising blood sugar levels.”"
