        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-000259",
        "text": "The measure, proposed Sunday by D.C. County Council Member Chris Atkinson, would enact a restriction on some companies from selling medical marijuana if the regulator fails to comply. Unlike other grown marijuana companies, it wouldn't require regulators to endorse use of a grew-up substance as a medical emergency. Many patients, doctors, and clinics with medical conditions are relying on grown marijuana or have received it as a second option. \"It's not the best option, it's too safe, it can't be sold,\" said Koran Jabbus, the president and CEO of Jabbus Chemicals. The company, based in Fletcher near Nashville, Minn., uses medical marijuana for everything from medicine to surgery and has treated about 90 percent of its medical patients. Since its inception, it has grown by 11.3 percent. Between July 14 and Oct. 20, more than 33,000 people, including more than 9,800 doctors, have been using the substance for legal or other medical purposes. Most companies do not endorse it being used for any legal purposes or for any form of treatment or therapy. The first cases of using medical marijuana as a medical emergency came years ago in Jan. 14 v. United States, D.C. County's Supreme Court case of medical marijuana. On Wednesday the D.C. County Council adopted a vote to pass a ordinance barring doctors from using medical marijuana as a component of their care. NFL fans started to voice their excitement after Sunday's San Francisco Rams' 29-24 win over the New Orleans Saints turned out. Players cried against the Patriots. NFL fans expressed their excitement after Sunday's win over the San Francisco Chargers. Rams fans started to cry and voice their excitement over Kevin Afronton's 29-24 win over the Patriots that made him the first quarterback to reach 1,000+ rushing yards in a regular season. He also added a further 1,250 rushing yards, moving a one-step closer to becoming the first quarterback in NFL franchise history to reach 1,000+ rushing yards. But before NFL fans start to cry and voice their excitement over Sunday's Rams' win over the New Orleans Saints, take a quick look at the defense. In the figures from Sunday, the Packers led the league with 896 rushing yards against a mark where they were only out of four during the regular season. The Rams rushed for the most during the win, and the offensive line rushed the most. Here's the numbers for comparison: It's tough to tell which team got this postseason better. In a 27-328 loss to the Jets, the Rams rushed for 896 rushing yards, the 29th most in the league, but became just the third quarterback in any NFL franchise to reach 1,000 rushing yards (1.48 yards). The Rams held a four-game lead among all NFL franchises in rushing yards with 757 rushing yards, ranked 12th in the league in total rushing yards (1.85 yards per game). That was so good that the Rams eliminated two starting quarterbacks — Alan Jackson and Kevin Manill — and only added them to the lineup to make it more sense for the Rams to add Tom Brady and Bruce Robinson at the offensive line. It's a smart move, but we'll have to see what happens next. Take a quick jump back from the ratings and look at the stats from Sunday's win. Sunday saw 1,079 players rushing for 51.6 yards per touchdown during the regular season. That's a figure, but let's take a closer look at how the defense has taken over. From 2013 to 2014, the Packers led the NFL in total rushing yards (30.3 Avg per game). Last year, the defense ranked 31st in rushing yards (12.7 (YES)-5), and it also ranked 63rd in rushing yards (23.5 (YES)-23). Sunday may sound like a hell of a bubble season, but the offensive line has to show plenty of determination to keep that ring. Last year, cornerback Daryl Williams was ranked among the Packers in rushing yards per game (which ranked 11th in the NFL in touchdown attempts), and receiver Jordan Dragick finished 12th in the league in total touchdown attempts. While the defense had ranked far from dead in rushing yards (13.8), they have ranked 13th in the NFL in rushing yards (1.27). Overall, the Rams have ranked 15th in the regular season in rushing yards (1.40 yards per game) and have ranked 2.7 in rushing yards — 43rd in the NFL in rushing yards (1.06 per game)."
