        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-000452",
        "text": "A person entered the home with a gun was shot. Authorities are looking for a suspect in an attack on a home on the 5000 block of South David Avenue early Saturday morning around 11:45 p.m., police said. The video footage showed a person entering the home without order after one of the guns fell, police said. The shooter was told on the property that two men had been involved, and that they had possessed a firearm, police said. The shooter was taken to the Cornberg Medical Center in Nevo where he died of minor injuries just after 10 p.m., police said. The shooter was taken to the Cornberg Medical Center in Nevo by an autotherapist where he was pronounced dead later, police said. “I want to thank the community for their compassion,” police said. The landlord of the attacked home was airborne to a nearby hospital and is in seriously critical condition, police said. The suspect in the attack is a woman whose home is owned by her parents. The home was filled with ammunition but not cash, police said. Authorities are currently looking for a suspect in an attack on a home on the 5000 block of South Carney Avenue, Jeremy Williamson, acting chief information officer, Denver police said. The video showed a person entering a home with a gun, but then a gun fell inside the home, causing the property to sneez, police said. “There was a lot of altercation going on,” Williamson told Nevo. No-one was injured at the scene, police said. Williamson. Authorities are currently looking for a suspect in an attack on a home on the 5000 block of South Carney Avenue, Jeremy Williamson, acting chief information officer, Denver police said. The suspect was a male student who went to work at Denver police. The announcement of the attack had sparked an almost immediate outpouring of concern from Denver residents, social media and the city’s executive branch over the weekend. The suspect was a male student who went to work at the Denver Police Department. No charges have been filed. The video was posted on YouTube and later circulated after a journalist was convicted in a New York police corruption case. Employees in Brooklyn, Denver, Denver, Delaware City, Delaware City, Evans City, Washington and Brooklyn counties pay minimum wages of 9.10 and 9.10 an hour on average, according to city statistics. The announcement of the attack had sparked an immediate outpouring of concern from Denver residents, social media and the city’s executive branch over the weekend. The suspect was a male student who went to work at the Denver Police Department. No charges have yet been filed. The video was posted on YouTube and circulated after a journalist from the city’s executive branch. President Donald Trump on Tuesday announced a dramatically modified version of the Affordable Care Act, which will extend health coverage to everyone who makes $225,000 a month, to everyone who makes over $10,000 a month and eventually to everyone who makes more than $50,000 a year, according to a press conference. One of the most serious consequences of the Affordable Care Act, the president said, is the loss of a health plan. The White House and the ACA are developing a modified version of the law, which will effectively eliminate government non-compliance, so that every American who signs up and purchases a health plan needs to add a share to their coverage, Trump told reporters during the announcement. The new provision would allow for individual health plan creators and buyers to add a smaller share to their insurance to extend their coverage. Instead of just removing an individual’s insurance share, consumers can use the share to cover additional costs, such as just the quality of their insurance and the length of their health plans. For example, every American who purchased a $2325,000 individual health plan in the period from 2011 to 2016 will have to add $500,000 to their coverage to extend their coverage. Individual-specific health plans, income-specific plans and family health plans are also included in the plan, Trump said. The Affordable Care Act will extend coverage to millions of Americans, mostly within the United States. Anyone who makes $10,000 a month will have to add $500,000 to their coverage. The plan also includes the income, individual and family tax breaks in other cities and states. These breaks are priced at a low rate on most low-wage earners, especially middle-class Americans. President Donald Trump wants to leave the tax breaks for millions of working Americans, and he wants to leave the tax breaks for all wealthy and working Americans and leave the tax breaks open at the local and state level. He wants to leave tax breaks for millions of working Americans, and he wants to leave the tax breaks open for all wealthy and working Americans."
