        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-000612",
        "text": "Police Chief Robert Carter says all drug cases should be considered in court and a lead review should be conducted. Updated Houston resident Michael Woods was arrested Monday afternoon on a major domestic violence charge involving the shooting and robbery that killed 499 people in Houston as well as others in the city, and officials are reviewing each resident's medical history. A man hasn't yet been charged against a driving-poo man in connection with the shooting of 499 people. Updated Houston resident Michael Woods has been arrested on a major domestic violence charge involving the shooting and robbery that killed 499 people in Houston as well as others in the city, and officials are reviewing each resident's medical history. (Published Monday 3) A man has been charged with five deaths in connection with his involvement in the shooting that killed three people and injured six others. Updated Police Chief Robert Carter says the five charges have been filed against a driving-poor man in connection with his involvement in the shooting of three people and injured six others. Police say the law needs to be changed around the country to rule out smoking, pot use, improvised motor vehicles and violence. \"As the mayor of our city, we are doing everything we can to ensure the enforcement of local drug laws,\" Carter said. Houston Police Chief David B. Goodall was speaking at a news conference Monday afternoon about the department's plans to deal with the drugs and calling for more aggressive investigations on the drugs. Houston Police Trooper Phil Anderson says two other people have now been charged in connection with the shooting that killed 499 people, a police officer and two other officers. He has not been charged in connection with the shooting that killed 499 people. The man has been under investigation for violations of police policy. Houston Police Trooper Phil Anderson says a fourth person has been charged in connection with the shooting that killed 499 people. (Published Monday 4) \"As mayor we are doing everything at our disposal to ensure the enforcement of drugs,\" Anderson said. Woods had been in the custody of a law enforcement officer who at the time of the deadly shooting in the 6600 block of De Mariza Ave. Woods opened fire on that officer and injured two other officers. Officials say Woods was just 20 at the time of the shooting. Police say he was on a bike during the incident. Woods is in police custody with the 31-year-old woman who was shot Monday in the 2200 block of West 46th Street. Police believe Woods asked her to steal the gun and then opened fire. Authorities don't believe Woods actually stole the weapon at the time of the shooting. Videos have surfaced showing Woods firing at the woman with a knife and speeding in the direction of two officers. A third video has surfaced showing two bi cyclists then opened fire and shot a third officer, killing a Houston law enforcement officer and a 31-year-old woman. The woman was shot Monday in the 1:25 block of West Bremson Ave. Woods has been in police custody with the woman who asked to shot dead and then opened fire. As Woods made his way to court, he spoke to police about why he was \"dripping marijuana\" and why he was not smoking marijuana with others when he was shot. Woods told police he has been smoking marijuana more than six to seven times since the shooting occurred. He also said he was smoking marijuana seven or eight times when the shooting occurred. Woods said he did not appear to smoke the drug at the time either the arrest or the shooting occurred. He also said he has not smoked any illegal substance between the time that he was arrested or the time that the shooting occurred. \"To be clear, I have signed a letter of consent for a year, and I cannot sign it again,\" Woods said. \"As an employee and the mayor of our city, we are doing everything we can to make sure the enforcement of local law does not include the use of drugs,\" he said. At a news conference Monday afternoon, Houston Police Department Chief David B. Goodall said the department is doing everything at its disposal to address the illegal substance issue in Houston which has grown since the shooting has resulted in nine officers deaths and three others having been injured. Goodall said the department will investigate such incidents and will work with local authorities to ensure officers don't use the drugs. In addition, Goodall said police will review any possible drug charges relating to the use of illegal substances. As part of an ongoing investigation, a crime review is ordered. Goodall said he won't be discussing any more crimes if he is unaware of any crimes. A crime review is ordered to ensure that police can improve policies and gather more information about such crimes."
