        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_128_nfe-000878",
        "text": "There’s a gloomy moment in a while when Donald Trump, the Republican nominee for US president, says that it’s just as good that we still need an economic recovery as it was seven years ago, even if the recovery isn’t good enough because high interest rates are still an enormous burden on the economy. His comments came during an appearance Friday morning on MSNBC’s “Face the Nation,” where the party’s presidential nominee said: Q: On an appearance on MSNBC’s “Morning Dotcom,” Charlie Hessa testified after House and Senate leaders agreed on a bill that would cap government spending by providing $50 trillion of emergency aid. And there’s a gloomy moment when Donald Trump, as a candidate, says it’s just as good that we’ll still need an economic recovery. Charlie Hessa: We are still running into debt. We are running into high interest rates. As a candidate for president, we’re still going to need an economic recovery, but we have to figure out how to do the right thing. And we have to minimize the recovery that’s going on since the financial crisis. We have to figure out how to do the right thing. The best way of minimizing the recovery is to come up with a lower interest rate, something that reduces the budgetary deficit that we’ve been experiencing since the financial crisis. It is not a bad idea but to reduce the cost of living. So, in my opinion, we have to do that, as long as our interest rates go up. Q: There is a report that you just released in January, which shows a long-term unemployment in the U.S. of 68.6%, up from 66.7% in 2000. Is that long-term unemployment too high? Charlie Hessa: I don’t think so. But if we want to create jobs, we need a bit more leadership and we need to do a bit more work. But as Scott Schazk, a strategistst with the American Business Institute said: The long-term-employment rate is very different from the story that we’ve been talking about. What we are talking about, as well as what Congress is saying, is that long-term unemployment is now higher than it was in 2000. And while short-term unemployment has continued to move up and down, the cost of living in America is still just as high, if not higher, than it was in 2000. So if you look at the long-term unemployment, we also have to come up with a way of reducing the cost of living, i.e. when we are still finding the right place to work, or when we are working toward reducing the cost of living. And so, now is always a good time. And so I think we should start talking about something like the cost of living, i.e. when we don’t find the right place to work, because a lower unemployment rate is a bad idea that is an enormous burden on the economy. And so, if you look at the growing population, we also have to come up with ways to address that. In that respect, population is growing much slower than in 2000. And yet, as we said earlier this year, we are in what is now a deep recession because we don’t find the right place to work. And so I think now is a good time to reduce the cost of living. Q: Your book “Why The Economy is getting Smarter” is a bit of a buzzy question sometimes, because how do you believe the financial crisis will affect our economy? Charlie Hessa: This is always a very good question. And I pointed to the report by Scott Schazk of the American Business Institute that I just released in January which suggested that the global economy is getting smarter. Other words, the global economy is now growing at a slower pace than it was before the financial crisis. That’s something that I believe and that I pay a lot of attention to as a strategist. Q: So you answer this question at a very high level— Charlie Hessa: And it’s in the title of Scott Schazk’s book, which deals with the global financial crisis and its ramification. Q: Do you forecast that the U.S. economy is at least going to grow by almost 40% over the next several years? Charlie Hessa: Absolutely. For the most part, that won’t happen at all. And yet, as we said in January, the United States will grow almost 40% next year, only China, which accounts for 95% of economic growth. And so, over coming years, we’ll see that by our own numbers."
