        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_64_nfe-000365",
        "text": "The drug has continued to decline in popularity, spurring some of the high numbers of users who use it every day. Nearly a quarter of potential users who have tried the drug have experienced pain symptoms from high amounts of heroin and cocaine. The declining health of cannabis over the past few years -- including pain, mood issues and substance consumption issues -- is a part of the problem. \"I think I'm tired of the idea of legalizing cannabis that hasn't got out of my mind,\" said Koshi Machi, a professor of pharmacology at the University of Brooklyn who has written about medical cannabis. \"The problem is that it cools people down. It is far too dangerous to put on government posters.\" Machi said it's up to the U.S. Congress to consult local doctors before deciding whether to legalize medical cannabis. \"It's up to the doctors to decide if it's too dangerous, and at its maximum potential to be.\" Medical cannabis sales showed a recent decline. Overall sales of more than 14 million plant-grown products rose by 33% in the second quarter of this year, up from 36% in February. \"The fruit market has been competing for ten to 11 years, and people are buying that far more,\" said Allan Kishman, Walmart.com's head of product sales. Jennifer McDonald, who operates a spice store in Pasadena, Calif., is buying some yuptines at her local California store. \"Everything I sell is different because of the plants I consume, which is partially the reason,\" said the California-based McDonald, who sells the plants, honey picks and raw fruit. Clinical biologist Michael Keels of the American Medical Association said the overall consumer usage of medical cannabis is at its lowest in 50 years. \"More 4 in 10 Americans would say that they are eating medical care cannabis right now,\" he said. Keels said the market is filling in, and there will still be around 150 million users. Police have detained around 80 Palestinians from parts of Israel, killing a 30-year-old man in a shoot-out. Police are investigating the death of 69-year-old Eel Shah, who was found Sunday in a home in the Mezi district in the Gaza city of Hama. No arrests so far have been made for him, but investigations are pending. Shah was in a critical condition at the time, when he was struck in the back of a Banana Street expressway and hit by a man in his car. He was pronounced dead Tuesday afternoon. \"We have detained a total of 80 Palestinians from different parts of Israel,\" said Yashman Hadas, spokesman at Hama's Public Hospital. \"The figures have not been confirmed yet, but we are still looking into the reasons and figuring out the facts,\" he said. Sustaicide rates hit record high in January In a statement Thursday, Mohammad Abqawa of the Israeli Health and Safety Ministry said 357 suicides had been reported during the month of January, rising to around 4,000. Israel had calculated figures based on the total population. He added that many of these cases were from Muslims and other ethnic groups. \", the suicides of this month have been absolutely unprecedented. The Israeli government has not been aware of the large number of cases reported so far. The government is not aware that so many suicides have been deemed necessary,\" he said, adding that Israel is conducting an investigation into the suicide rates of Palestinians reported in January. Abdel Harman, deputy secretary of the Health and Safety Ministry of Israel, said people should watch with caution \"if they are confined in Israel for a limited period\" by a Palestinian suicide. \"Israel authorities are willing to take risks to guarantee the safety of all Palestinian residents.\" He said Israeli authorities should review a Palestinian law which passed in Congress last week, which removed a legal obligation of confessing suicide. Officials should educate Palestinians in Israel on the legal and moral aspects of the death penalty, \"the safety of the residents of Israel and the safety of all Palestinians,\" he said. WASHINGTON President Donald Trump called on Congress to review the accuracy of the reports from his mother of the attack and death of a 13-year-old boy. On Tuesday, Trump tweeted that his mother has refused to talk to him if their son had died this year: \"He's talking to his dad, and I don't get that,\" he told CNN's Doug Stringer. \"You know, he is not talking to me,\" he said in an interview. \"If he can kill a little boy, he's going to have to talk to his dad.\""
