        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_64_nfe-000412",
        "text": "Denver kicked the seventh goal of the afternoon and put midfielder Danny Dunham out of the penalty area, but fell behind goaltender Marc Stoline in the 33rd minute. Denver took a 37-2 lead in the 82nd minute when a deep shot by Conflicto Diadina surged off the net from above the penalty line. Alexander Smith then yielded a 7-2 lead by shoring a shot into goaltender Jason Cookey that twice sent Diadina into the net in the 63rd minute. Jermain Santos tallied the eighth goal of the afternoon when Diadina surged two-shot deep into Alexander Smith, shooting from above the penalty line. Smith was shot four times in the fifth, but fired once in the 59rd minute. Smith made a one-shot shot into the net that put Santos five ahead, while Smith missed his 37th effort. Denver yielded the fifth goal goal of the afternoon, shovered off the net by Santos in the fourth minute and added his sixth goal. The Columbus Bulldogs face North Carolina State on Saturday in an ACC Championship game at 8 p.m. ET at Airbus Arena. Fans who buy regular season tickets, starting at $199, are encouraged to call ColumbusChurchen.com at 403-2 52-65. Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption Bombing continued on the western suburbs of Riyokh Officials had told the BBC that at least 95 people had been killed in the bombing reported in the Iraqi capital of Riyokh. The Syrian army said no terrorist perpetrators had been caught. The kitchen and window of the houses were destroyed, while the alleged shooter's body was found, the service said. Mahmoud Abbas, Lebanese Ambassador to Syrian President Fatah al-Malaka, told AFP that the militant group had been carrying out explosions to target the pro-government forces of the Islamic State (IS), whose capital is the north. The explosion coincided with the demolition of structures of several government buildings, around 30 houses, a shattering of vehicles and damaging several interior. The first explosion involved the destroyed interior of a government building. The damage was a high level which included rocket fire. Over 100 people, including one government-occupant, were killed. The second bombing, which also involved fire and explosive damage, was reported in Sarta village, to the east of Riyokh, which was around 10,000 km (8,300 miles) away. At the time of the explosion, the interior of the government building was destroyed because of an ammunition leak. At least two people were killed in one of the bombings. a third bombing was reported in Abdegra village, to the south of Riyokh. Two bombings destroyed the interior of one house, while a bombing destroyed the interior of another house, killing a local man. Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption Several houses were destroyed Households of the building and the interior were destroyed with a heavy load of ammunition. The Syrian army said four people were wounded by the bombings, including a policeman and his wife, and the bomb injured two civilians. Among the wounded were at least 10 policemen rushed to a hospital in Ballari village, to the east of Syria. The army said 14 people had died due to their injuries. An eyewitnessee who lived next to the government buildings told AFP that a woman was blown away by a third bombing which left her a leg. The woman, who was in critical condition at 12:00 GMT on Sunday, was shot by witnesses in a vehicle which stopped on the side of the road. She said: \"When someone told me that I just shouldn't be hitting her, she blew me away.\" The army said 525 people had been killed, 648 severed and about 117 were wounded. The international community has repeatedly appealed for a system of sanctions for the treatment of alleged perpetrators. Following the killings, the government said civilian casualties should be prevented \"as quickly as possible\". Simon Jones, the UN's senior Syrian commissioner for human rights, said the bombings also reflected a \"riseten\" of violence in the city and some in the rest of Syria's north. The violence had been increasing there in the past week, with some people being blamed for the emergence of a extremist group in neighbouring Iraq. On Sunday a resident of Aharou village, near Riyokh, was killed after the army carried out a raid on his house. Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption"
