        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_64_nfe-000721",
        "text": "The shooting, near Walker, south of Oakland, resulted in at least nine deaths. It was followed by a flood of gun threats and threats on social media, including on television programs such as “The Tonight Show” and “Fake News,” and sitcoms such as “Politics TV,” officials said. The extent of the shooting was not immediately cleared, and identities of the suspects are unknown. A 71-year-old man was taken to the hospital after being found dead inside a van owned by a correctional company on Stockton Beach on Thursday. Harding was later found with a bullet wound on the back seat of the van, Stockton Beach sheriffs officials said. Harding was in the van when deputies arrived to take him to another van owned by another employee, who had been in his office. One employee, who knocked the door into Harding's dining room, told him to leave. He said the men were sitting at one of their favorite restaurants when Harding was found dead with four gun wounds between them. Seconds later, Harding was found inside the van, Deputtee County Sheriff Steve Smith said. \"This other employee was sitting right inside the van,\" Smith said. Detectants initially reported that Harding was sitting at the restaurant located in a downtown corner. They also found marijuana, cocaine, pain-killers, muscle suppressrs and other drugs inside the van, officials said. Once they reached the restaurant where Harding was staying, rescuers entered the van, picked up the drugs, and opened fire. \"Everyone heard the van driving and saw it flying into the forest,\" said David Kishner, a sheriff's deputy who was driving at a traffic stop close to the beach. When the vehicle dragged by, the van ran out of control and fired twice before it crashed to the ground, officials said. Afterward, they said, deputies finally arrived to look for suspects and interview witnesses. Around 6:30 a.m., deputies also saw Taukya Harding sitting down at a residential goods market on the 300 block of Keesbeach Road. They found Harding and his wife, who said she was sitting holding her cannon and was sitting on a glass chair in front of her. \"She was just holding it up and telling him to sit next to her,\" Kishner said. \"It was like grabbing a button.\" Deputies also asked deputying officials to record a 911 call about Harding's death. The next morning, that call was repeated and the call could not be returned, due to the intense violence. When officials finally arrived, Kishner found Harding's body in the dining room and reported that he had been taken to the hospital. \"He just died in the middle of a dining room from a gunshot wound wound,\" Kishner said. \"He has been mourning his death since he died,\" the officials said. When Harding was found dead, deputies gathered at a restaurant around 11 a.m. to find his body. They also found his body along a road, which is where Harding once lived, officials said. When deputies arrived the next morning after finding Harding's body, he ran out on a chase. They continued looking for the man thought to be a key suspect in the case. At one point, deputies were not able to speak to Harding since he had never been seen. But they soon began a careful search for suspects, which found what officials initially thought might be the reason Harding was killed. For instance, around 5 a.m., as Harding was sitting at the table at one of the restaurants, he fired four rounds down Carter Avenue. \"I pulled him down the table and shot him as if he had a shotgun,\" Kishner said. Approximately an hour later, Harding's family told deputies that they were looking for bankcards and birth certificates before they were shot, Kishner said. Deputies arrived the restaurant with a final look for food the next morning. They found Harding sitting at his desk, behind the restaurant. \"He was walking around town shopping,\" restaurant manager Robert Merkoff said. \"He was not eating, he was not feeling good, he was wearing a lot of shoes and shoes.\" (Reporting by Lisa B. Shane; Editing by Tina Williams; Editing by Nataliea Gamski) Content created by The Daily Caller News Foundation is available without charge to any eligible news publisher that can provide a large audience. For licensing opportunities for our original content, please contact licensing@foundation.org"
