        "id": "owt-owt_v2_elf_m_64_nfe-000883",
        "text": "Unite and take part in an epic battle with the enemys. Not only will you defeat the armies, but the player will determine your ultimate advantage amongst the opponents. You'll also try to limit the progress of the opposing armies to make the epic more exploring and engaging. If you have played the game of Prince Citizen for years, then you’re happy to have a newly released software version of Prince Citizen: Beta 2.0. As previously mentioned, Beta 2.0 now features a dramatically improved gameplay experience from its predecessors. Players will now be able to solve and solve problems, collect resources, explore, and refine the game to the greatest extent possible. An important thing to note is that the AFO has been releasing several major additions to the classic game, such as dozens of new pre-fixs and jump modes. The AFO has also been making improvements to the classic Prince Citizen experience, including improved jumping techniques, improved movement, and speed. Aside from adding new pre-fixs, new jump modes, etc, these latest additions now stand vastly apart from the original game, providing you with a faster, smoother experience once you defeat your enemys. There are four new jump modes: 1 (which allows you to lift yourself to the ground), 2 (allowing you to progress), 3 (can you accelerate yourself up), 4 (can you even lift yourself back). These are all free to download, so don’t be disappointed to even try them! Beta will launch for Mac and Linux. Got any other features you’d like adding to this game? Hit us on Twitter, or Facebook page and we’ll let you know if we’ve got any ideas. Here are news releases: Around 6:20 p.m., a 36-year-old man was shot from the stomach by two men in the 300 block of South Bowen Avenue, according to Ninth-Park Police Police. The victim confronted the man who put two bullets at the top of his stomach and fired three bullets into the air. The man hit the man in the stomach and began shooting at the victim, police said. Arrest reports indicate the two men surrendered and the man was still waiting for his gun to surrender. The men grabbed the victim and told the man that he had non-lifely-threatening injuries, police said. The victim was handed a weapon. He was treated at the hospital. No injuries from the shooting have been reported, according to Orange Park police. Around 7 p.m., a man was accidentally shot off the top of his stomach as three men stopped by a car in the 300 block of South North Island Avenue, according to Ninth State Park police. There was a shootout between the two men. Neither men was shot accidentally, although no injuries were reported. Around this time, a man who shot a 44-year-old woman was admitted to the hospital and is currently being evaluated for non-lifethreatening injuries, police said. His two bullets were found on the top of his stomach and no injuries were reported to police. None of the injuries involved a life threat from his opening fire. At approximately 9:20 p.m., a suspect was shooting at a get-on-the-ground gun show in the 3500 block of South Greenwood Avenue. The suspect was carrying a firearm when he opened fire, which hit the other suspect, police said. Arrest reports indicate the suspect also had non-life-threatening injuries. He was treated at the hospital. The suspect fled about five minutes after the shooting occurred and has since been under investigation, according to Orange Park police. The suspect was taken into custody while reports of his non-life-threatening injuries were received, although they could not be confirmed to police. Here are news releases from Orange Park police: Around the same time, a 32-year-old woman was fatally shot by another bystander at Orange Park police. The woman was treated at the hospital, according to news releases from Orange Park police. A time earlier, a 33-year-old man was accidentally shot by a ham officer in the 4200 block of North Fanny Avenue. The victim was faced with three bullets before opening fire. The officer shot the man in the leg, killing the victim. Police believe it was a random incident involving \"a random witness,\" but have yet to identify the attacker. \"I don't know how he could speak because he hit the officer so hard,\" said Orange State Park Police Chief Cody Simmons. He said he believes the incident was a \"major\" shooting and though he believes police can trace down the shooter, he didn't see any footage yet. \"I think it's definitely a major shooting to honesty.\""
