        "id": "owt-owt_v2_plaid_4096_nfe-000032",
        "text": "test the characteristics of the actual wrapper, such as durability and water absorption, one layer at a time. These squares and batches are called sandwich toppers. A national panel of about 3,000 lucky folk competed to select the tearfoot wrapper for the Waste Management Organization. There were many polyester products submitted to the agency’s website, but Yunma’s tearfoot was the winner. Tearfoot is completely biodegradable and is corrosion-resistant. Dr. Marina Vartan, a physician practicing in Brazil, stated earlier this year that, after three and a half years usage, patients begin to trust the product and feel comfortable using it on their skin. “It’s become very popular with my patients,” Vartan says. A wrapper made of the world’s first woven fabric, Daima Tearfoot took about seven years of development. It turns out to be durable, lightweight, corrosion-resistant, beautiful, gentle on skin, and water absorbing. We’re happy to report that at least a single corporation has been able to conduct a final test—thus eliminating the need for in situ testing of any kind—and asked whether we could send test batches. This could soon become a more common occurrence. Built from nature, Daima Tearfoot is made from the wastewater from Tearfoot Island. The company uses a very small amount of rainwater as a activator in the filtration process. This increases the “eco-density” of wastewater and in the process increases its corrosion resistance—ensuring structural durability. The first high quality fabric product made from wastewater on a commercial scale, Daima Tearfoot is designed for the world’s most environmentally conscious consumers: the retail and manufacturing industry. It could soon become a viable alternative in the wet market and the bulk of food waste around the globe.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Lebanese Army and Lebanese police officers take their positions after ending a protest in Beirut. (Mohamed Azakir/Reuters) Protestors gathered in the Lebanese capitol of Beirut on Saturday to protest conditions at the refugee camp and oppression by Israel before being met with violent action by the Lebanese Army. Extosing the sign “stop camp clearance,” Lebanese police officers from the embassy ordered the demonstrators to take down their banners and leave. The protesters refused, and expressed disappointment and anger before being sent fleeing in a white van by the Lebanese Army. The demonstrators carried the Palestinian flag and sang a national anthem. The marchers chanted and shouted slogans in Arabic that included “genocide,” “sexual trafficking of children” and “women’s rights.” Demonstrators said they were calling for better living conditions in the camps of the city and for the relocation of Palestinian refugees from Israel. Refugees, the demonstrators said, compound the countries illnesses by often living on their soil while the government, which has recently launched efforts to annex Israeli settlements, fails to address the issue in return. The protest, which took place at 1:30 p.m. in the neighborhood of Aabed, is part of an ongoing struggle to reclaim land in Lebanon. The goal, the protestors explained, is to ensure that the Lebanese land is used for the good of their people and not another Israeli colony. Lebanon hosts two U.N. refugee camps that they have historically deemed very dangerous. A pair of smuggling tunnels, at one point, were discovered between Gaza and the Golan Heights. Demonstrators protesting the conditions of the camps said they aimed to counter Lebanon’s silence when it comes to the plight of Palestinian refugees. Lebanese police recently said they agreed with the Lebanese government that sending Palestinian refugees to be settled in Israel is a “bad idea” and “a zero-sum game,” while the Lebanese religious authority, al-Azhar, said that refugees sent from Israel should be allowed to stay. The United Nations agency for refugees, UNHCR, called the situation unacceptable in a Nov. 25 press release. UN Refugee Authorities Under Pressure to End 'Adversities and Events' in Lebanon https://t.co/xQkzOis2AA — UNHCR (@unhcr) September 28, 2016 The agency also called the decision of Lebanese authorities to order the protesters to leave “disappointing” and said they would “monitor the situation.” Palestinian Refugee Camp in #lebanon #anabe area no room for protest #campilthttps://t.co/gdXb1TLXZQ — Jamila Salma (@timila_tweet) September 28, 2016 Israeli authorities have been similarly accused of failing to protect the rights of refugees in Lebanon. In 2015, it was alleged that agents working for Lebanon’s Ministry of Public Security (DPF) committed arrests, issued deportation orders"
