        "id": "owt-owt_v2_plaid_4096_nfe-000651",
        "text": "this summer. Pedro will start 2014 at Short-A Carolina and possibly in the Atlantic League. Mills will start the 2014 season in the starting rotation at Double-A Carolina after spending 2013 starting at Triple-A Durham Dunn will start the 2014 season at Triple-A Durham after spending 2013 starting at High-A Mobile Jones will start the 2014 season as a utility/front bench player at Double-A Carolina after spending 2013 at High-A Frederick Note: Please keep in mind these Braves prospects could still make a big impact in the majors in 2014 at their current ages, big prospects with potential to contribute for the first time at the MLB level. Who are the prospects? Who are the FAVORITES? Signed Fantarly Zach Aviles entering the 2014 season in the system (Righthole pitcher): Aviles has racked up 24 or more strikeouts in each season the past two years and pitched 68 relief innings in the 2013 season after pitching 107 in 2012. The 21-year-old finished with a 3.60 ERA (6.2 K/9 in 2013 and a 7.0 K/9 in 2012) and an above-average walk rate. He's regarded as one of the top-tier pitching prospects in the system, though the lack of quality pieces in the system will put pressure on the bullpen and the bullpen. Aviles has two years left on his short-term contract and will start next season as a starting pitcher, though he will likely move to relief due to a control issue or season-ending injury at some point in the future. Zach Nakarz Top prospect in the system (Starting, first base, Closer and Backup catcher, third base): The 22-year-old southpaw has been one of the best starters in the Minor Leagues the past two seasons, posting a 2.33 ERA (9.4 K/9 in 2013 and 9.2 K/9 in 2012) and a track record to boot. He's done this despite slightly below-average home run prevention (0.8 HR/9 in 2013 and 1.8 HR/9 in 2012) and high walk rates (4.7 BB/9 in 2013 and 5.9/9 in 2012). He has allowed 5.0 BB/9 on average during his professional career, though such results are not always sustainable in the Majors, as he's had his share of high-swinging teammates that have dragged down his fly ball numbers. Natz has another year left on his current deal and will likely start next season as a closer or maybe make a closer move somewhere down the line. Justin Smoak entering the 2014 season in the system (Shortstop, Left field, third base): The speedy infielder has been an absolute success in his first two MLB seasons, hitting .276/.357/.387 with 33 home runs and 236 RBI across 853 at bats at the minor league level. His offense has slowed significantly with the jump to the Major Leagues, as he has played to a .260/.313/.367 slash line at those levels the past two years. A lingering elbow injury slowed his production down even further this year to just 24 games in 2013. While some scouts consider his bat highly, some strongly loathe his bat and think he doesn't get inside fast enough. Don't be surprised if you see him used as a starting shortstop in 2014. Smoak has another year left on his current deal and will most likely spend the season primarily at third base. Bryan Wong Top prospect in the system: The 21-year-old, athletic right fielder hit .262/.289/.430 with 12 home runs, 30 stolen bases, and 76 RBI across 425 at bats at the minor league level. Not quite good enough to warrant long looks in right field, but certainly raw enough that he's unlikely to ever have much of an impact outside of second base. Ronald Guanagni entering the 2014 season in the system (Center Field, Right Fielder, Backup catcher): The 21-year-old right fielder was a bit of a surprise as to the reaction of Braves fans when he was selected in the 2013 MLB Draft. Described by scouts as a defensive standout, many were surprised to see him selected by the Braves. Guanizzi is truly the wild card here but could certainly be a force in the outfield. Noah Seager Top prospect: Seager is a second baseman who was drafted by the Phillies in 2011 with the 50th overall pick. At 6-3 and 225 pounds, he has great size for his position and is a solid defender off the bench. His lack of power in his bat has been a big problem for the Braves, though coaching should fix the problem early in his career. He has a lot of athleticism and will probably mash catchers as"
