        "id": "owt-owt_v2_plaid_4096_nfe-000742",
        "text": "as a blinking light. It could also act as a “whisper” and be modulated through haptics. The invention (entitled patent) also envines… …a means for this device to unfold and take form as a wearable device. Other than controlling “the user’s motions” it could have additional capabilities such as providing “information about the user” and “increased amplility”. The interface would be worn in the hand of an adult user with the modules expansion frequencies including… …a heating flow which allows for the heat flow to be adjusted to achieve a higher temperature using a predetermined “heart rate of the body” and a “non-adhesive surface” which can be applied to objects on the body. A secondary display… …could access information as the current status of the interface, the sleep onset (with a varying strength and sleep duration), and the data for the audio-visual interface. It also it is possible to add variations in identifying particular locales to which “electronic device information” can be sent.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>April 3, 2014 – The tussle in Lincoln Park between the protesters and the police took a bizarre turn Tuesday when a member of the Black Lives Matter movement was mistaken for one of the protesters, leading police to violently arrest him during the demonstration. Trinh Tran 21, a student leader at Chicago State University, has been following BLM Protesters since the march after the president’s Chicago address. Tran said he was observing a “sympathetic protest” in front of cameras and happened to face a large giant TV filled with protesters. Tran did not expect to be immediately surrounded by two police officers in a waiting armored van. “I did not see people in my eyes, coming at me with a lot of force,” he said. He added, “I did not expect to be put in a police chokehold.” He did not anticipate that the pepper spray (the kind Chicago has used on many of the Occupy student arrestees) fired by the police would go directly in his eyes. He said that the gas did not get deep in his eyes or ears, but the amount used did give him “a numbness of sorts, a burning sensation” to the back of the head. “I had to place a 2-inch bandage in my left eye to draw blood because I was in shock because of the amount of force I got hit with,” he said. “Although only a minute or so, it was very invasive I must say because I was not in a close-view scanner. I knew they were doing circles in my eyes because of the pepper spray.” The student protesters’ group was outraged. “The police are responsible for putting sticks on protesters who are suspected of having weapons as this high-powered weapon has been primarily used to mow down unarmed civilians like Mike Brown,” he said. “We think this should be used on criminals or domestic offenders, not on a group of protesters who are trying to disrupt the speech.” Tran, who is a chapter leader for the Black Student Union, organized a group of protesters that were taken away in a drag pen and subdued with sticks by riot police. Tran believes he was arrested because he was a troublemaker who showed up to a peaceful protest. “They are obviously consider me a troublemaker simply because I am representing Mike Brown, who was killed at gunpoint by a white CPD officer,” he said. The arrested protesters, meanwhile, are suing the university: The University of Chicago’s mass arrest against a peaceful Black Lives Matter protester last week, Trinh Tran, was a “premeditated” act by the police and therefore a crime, the Black Student Union claims in a lawsuit against the school. The last-minute arrest came during the post-address demonstration at Obama’s speech, in which he gave a much-awaited address about slain African-American teenager Michael Brown, who was fatally killed by a police officer in 2014. The protests erupted immediately after Obama’s speech, lasting for nine minutes, and while the police stopped the mob from straying, it eventually began to cross paths with Tran, who is a member of the university’s Black Student Union. Fifty-one people, including five journalists, were arrested in the melee. Among them was Tran, who posted a video of his arrest on Facebook and was handed a six-day suspension for walking out of the speech. Tran ended up fighting the suspension in a courtroom and eventually got it reduced to three days. The whole episode was described as “chaotic” by someone who witnessed it. The university admits in a sixty-page complaint, now publicly available on the union’s website, that “there was no justification” for Tran’s arrest and that two officers were never disciplined for their crimes. From the complaint: “No attempt was made to remove"
