        "text": "private gatherings inside nursing homes appear to be in violation of the governor's emergency declaration. In Massachusetts, Massachusetts federal courts are now weighing whether the state ban on staying at home is a valid emergency measure. Health care professionals and senior residents in group homes worry that sending vulnerable residents outdoors would increase the risk of their contracting the virus. \"Operationally, it doesn't make sense,\" said Sylvia Abramichich, a group home resident and native of Rhode Island, who also lives at home with her parents. \"You have everything from the elderly to her toddler in the bedroom, a bathroom and a shower. It just doesn't make sense.\" But concerns about privacy-a concept that all Rhode Island residents hold to be sacred-and older residents' daily needs cannot be ignored, especially at this unprecedented period in human history. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends minimizing close contact with others, unless under the supervision of a healthcare professional and practicing social distancing when possible. CDC also lists activities that large groups of people should avoid doing to avoid spreading the virus, including gathering in the same place, washing their hands quickly, avoiding off-the-counter medications and washing their hands before use. The Group Home Association, the national trade association for group homes, says that imposing limits on visitors reflects the balance between maintaining safe communities while protecting vulnerable residents. It also does not support an outright ban on external visits at this time. \"In order to minimize the spread of the disease in group homes, we support the CDC guidance recommending the avoidance of groups of more than 10 people,\" GHA spokesman Scott Phillips said. \"We are not advocating a total ban on visits. The point is that residential centers should be screening visitors and requiring their residents to stay indoors, just as health care facilities have used [patient screening] for a long time.\" The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) has warned school administrators against sending children and teenagers outside on their own for prolonged periods of time. And public health officials are advising older people to stay at home except if they need medical care. Both the Rhode Island AHA and the Federal National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health (NIOSH) have issued regulations urging Americans to practice good hygiene and engage in social distancing and reduce physical gatherings to curb the spread of the deadly virus. Those restrictions may soon be lifted in some states, allowing Americans to congregate in close quarters and engender in the spread of the virus. \"It personally concerns me,\" said AHA President Larry M. Bortz, a graduate of Rhode Island State College and an AHA national board member. \"I'm aware that many states are looking at new legislation to allow assisted living facilities to operate in some capacity.\" New Island's state guidance states that owners should take appropriate precautions for safe accommodations. The beds should be under six feet in size, or approximately 3.5 feet by 9.5 feet with 5.5 feet of distance drywall between the beds, with a hallway in the middle of each bed. It also advises owners that their group homes must feature private dining areas, play rooms that can accommodate children and small couches where residents can lie together. Beds should be placed as far as possible from open spaces, and residents should only be allowed to open their room to a visitor when asked. NIOSH state policy director Jeryl King emphasized that group homes should not be confused with nursing homes. \"A group home is any type of housing that has single occupancy, that has closed, private individual bed rooms on private property,\" King said. \"People sort of amputate what those type of homes are, but in the AHA's definition, they are the only type that are for single occupancy.\" Both Rhode Island's guidance and the associations agree that keeping residents and visitors separated is necessary to protect the safety of older residents, particularly those who need assistance with daily activities. Both state and federal guidelines allow external visits to take place in assisted living facilities, but only when private visitors have permission from residents. At independent living facilities like group homes, visitors have to be escorted by law enforcement. Those who do not leave when asked could be arrested. \"We know of one individual who was allowed to visit one resident in a townhouse property with no advance arrangement,\" Phillips said. \"The resident was accompanied by a security guard but did not open the property for personal use. We do not believe the resident was charged.\" Judy Gallivan, president of the Rhode Island Group Home Association, insisted on the need to educate residents who she felt are considering resistance to the state's executive order. \"You really can't go to an independent living facility and ask them why they're not open,\" she told NewsLive. Gallivan also encountered cynicism and mistrust from residents on her own group home visits to disinfect"
