        "id": "owt-owt_v2_plaid_4096_nfe-000801",
        "text": "IMO) can lay out the tasks and collect initial information on the project execution to formulate a solid plan quickly. Co-manager The project master project manager (PMPM) is primarily responsible for galvanizing the rest of the team to guarantee success of the entire project execution. By working with the other key team members, the PMPM can improve as project performance, maintain transparency of the project process, and also provide continual guidance to his or her team. Contractors Firm-Stage management The stakeholder firm who signed the contract will depend on the project phase manager to keep the execution moving forward with time. “Your project phase manager actually takes charge of the project management and operational decisions. This person can serve as the project, design, systems, or senior program manager, or serve as a contractor or prime contractor.” Under the typical PPA agreement in practice, there is one part of the team that will work on the project full-time and another part of the team that will work on the project part-time. However, specific roles vary. Managing Collaborative Management The main purpose of this role is to work on the project activities that are performed by the rest of the team to make sure the entire project is successfully executed. The most important goal of this position is to assess the ROI on the project activity and the overall enterprise planning. IT tasks related to the Project Manager(s) It is important to maintain project information and metrics to keep up with the latest actions and to properly report on any delays in the project. “… a project manager should regularly gather all of the relevant information needed to assess the overall progress and deliver on the project goals. They should also manage the roadmap and come up with innovative ways to effectively achieve the objectives.” Technical Inspector One role that this manager performs is to properly monitor the entire project’s scope of work and make sure that the customers are getting the maximum value for the money as stated in the contract. In addition, they should supervise the project for performance and manage the post-project management. Basically, if you have a technical inspector to monitor the project for success, don’t let them supervise the whole project management. “The assistant should also monitor the project’s scope of work for available work and support staff, and prepare audit documentation and statistical reports for each project.” Strategic Analyst There are several aspects to strategic analysis. One is to estimate costs of the overall project and ensure that the cost model, business case, and therefore the budget is prepared for project delivery. Another one is to identify assets and estimate optimal asset allocation in the portfolio. In essence, if you don’t have assets, you don’t have money. Financial Analyst This is where financial consultants come into play. In addition to advising and helping with procurement, this consultant can: Create cost models and strategic ROI analyses for one or multiple activities throughout the project life; Execute independent financial analysis to determine optimal outsourcing options; and Assess the overall effectiveness of various levels of ROI. “Choosing a practitioner of independent financial analysis. This can be a member of the executive team, an operational specialist designated by the general manager, or an independent expert in financial accounting, program management, systems management, program implementation, procurement modeling or analysis, applied manufacturing, mechanical engineering, or any other appropriate discipline.” Conclusion Darrell Gerard, CPA, and Built Group Consultant, puts this into perspective: “Financial accounting is a strategic discipline. Through analysis, procurement, and planning, you can ensure that all of the assets necessary to generate an ROI are allocated and used in the right way. This requires a thorough and holistic management that takes into account business objectives to effectively take advantage of these valuable assets and achieve strong ROEs.” Lessons learned during the Agile Project Management can be applied to help manage the execution of both small projects and large projects in an effort to deliver value for money and resources.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>“Them all by the n-word, they are . . . 350.” Kobe Bryant has been very busy the past few days. The brilliant performance in the NBA Finals and the mostly entertaining but unthreatening Shaq saga probably deserve more attention. But as usual, one brief piece at least made the day’s news carry more meaning. Advertisement Journey from Davidson Star to Star: Milestones from a Talented Young Kobe Bryant Advertisement The photo appeared in the debut issue of SI magazine on January 28, 1973, page one. Bryant stands at the 2 guard; he played the position during his days at Indiana University. Clifton Anderson and Bob Schultz, who worked for SI’s magazines at that time, photographed Bryant in the"
