        "text": "we have reached that point. It is a matter of timing.\" 'Tolerable' Mr Osborne added that he had been clear with voters during the election campaign that he planned to cut the annual deficit to less than half of the deficit under whoever won power. \"My expectation is that we'll find ourselves even further away from that than I want it to be - I think we will send a bad signal to the markets if we try to do it,\" he said. \"So we'll make the necessary spending cuts, but try to make them in a sensible way, and try to strike a balance between protecting our economy in the short run and trying to return to growth in the long run.\" After nine years of balanced budgets, some growth in borrowing was inevitable, he said, adding: \"Much of it will be borrowed. \"This is where I want us to be - I need to return to the penny. Deficits will be bigger, but not as small as I want them to be.\" Asked if there would be any rise in interest rates, in light of the concerns over the size of the deficit, he said: \"Yes, my assumption is that interest rates will go up but we will be very careful in the cuts we are making.\" Business secretary Toby Young said: \"So David Cameron will have to work on the assumption that interest rates will remain at current levels... He's repeatedly told the FYIPS he's not taking any risks with any repeat of the capital gains tax fiasco.\" Live Topics Press Association<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|> This gorgeous ring may be your next soul companion. Wrapping about one-fourth of a human’s length, this bracelet, which has already sold out on Kickstarter, is mesmerizingly beautiful. The fabric of the bracelet is satin, a unique type of vegetable fiber that has, among other benefits, contributed to the beauty of the perfect skin. When curled into a square and balanced between your fingers, the bracelet takes on the shape of a flag, and pins you squarely in the face. Via Mysterious Minds. PHOTO 1: PICTURE 2: PICTURE 3 (A close-up):<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>A SCANTLY tattooed man threatened to slap police in the face with a \"face boot\" in a prisoner as he became unhinged, a court has heard. Terry Dempsey claimed he did not know what he was doing after attacking an officer who was watching over a safety assessment exercise he was invited to take part in through the welfare agency. The 42-year-old has been held at Hilton Motels and is on bail after being arrested during the 20-minute exercise at Bidock Stables, near Yarama, on September 21. Dressed in a T-shirt and wearing his adult trousers, Dempsey, who waived his plea, held his hand towards his lips as he appeared in the District Court. He pleaded not guilty to three charges of assaulting a police officer and one charge of squeezing his fists. Sergeant Paul Smith SC, prosecuting, said the accused was \"excessively violent\" and had \"really not had a stable life\". He said the accused had no criminal record, but had a previous history of violence which included a machete attack where he stabbed a victim in the neck and his violent escape after police used pepper spray on a 33-year-old woman, leaving her with black eyes and sore skin. Another witness said Dempsey threw a plastic box at her after police arrested him in March 2015 for pulling down an emergency pole. Two witnesses said they saw Dempsey as community support workers attempted to restrain him after they issued him a walking pass to take part in the safety assessment exercise. Sgt Smith told news.com.au that Dempsey attempted to mislead by emailing Sergeant Silbo prior to his arrest \"with completely different language which completely mischaracterised what he was doing and why he assaulted the officer\". The court was also told that the welfare claimant assaulted police because he did not know what he was doing and he was \"afraid of his own reflection\". The officer told the court that he was forced to issue the walking pass as he had \"seen some issues with abusive and violent behaviour\" during the walk-in safety assessment at the Grammar Behaviour and Assessment Centre at the Department of Human Services in Toowoomba. Sentencing the accused, District Judge John Palmer said that Dempsey's behaviour had \"deteriorated\" dramatically and displayed an \"impermissible emotional instability that speaked into unprovoked offensive and potentially violent behaviour\". He ordered him to be sentenced within 12 weeks where it is expected that he will remain engaged in serious criminal behaviour for"
