        "id": "owt-owt_v2_plaid_4096_nfe-001004",
        "text": "-energy economy. [2] Electricity production analyses have revealed the different energy expenditures that are required to produce commodities depending on the scale different crops are grown at and the use of various agricultural inputs. The diagram below, using the area of a corn and soybean field, illustrates the amount of electricity each common crop input would need to directly produce. [3] Thus electricity consumption analyses can be used to estimate not just production costs, but the crop and agricultural energy expenditures required to produce commodities, a metric that can enable us to measure the efficiency and sustainability of our production activities across the agricultural sector. It will take into account expenditures that are currently allowed to take place in the industry and the energy input they represent. Assumptions of energy Most crops we produce need to have completed their life cycle long before getting to market. This process is largely dependent on the quality of the soil, vegetation, and local conditions. Each commodity can go through unique growth stages that require a unique agricultural input. When we talk about energy, one of the most commonly used reference metrics is Mileage Eqution Efficiency (FFE), which measures how much energy is required to drive a truck from the airport to the drive-thru. As the name implies, we can apply this metric to the yield of a product, measured by the total energy needed to produce it per unit of labor (man-hours) and materials (land, water, etc.) used. Diagram of a pre-production system. Image from farmoftheplanet.com. While generally MTEs are very similar between products made in the Pacific Northwest and those made in Georgia or West Virginia, they are very different between products made in California or New York and those made in Mexico. Each commodity, however, has unique growth stages that require a unique agricultural input, which vary based on the characteristics of the soil and vegetation. These differences can be measured by combining the agricultural electricity input to produce commodities with the metabolic value. Thus, the calculation takes into account electricity expenditures at harvest, levels of crop productivity and differences in levels of moisture to facilitate growing a given crop variety. The calories used in the commodity are then divided by the crop's metabolic value, a quantity that reflects the average energy consumed in producing agricultural products. To determine a metabolic value, long field-trails take place. Single crops alone are not enough to calculate energy expenditures at harvest. If we replace a field of alfalfa, maize, cotton or corn with neighboring fields of corn, wheat, soybean or wheat then the level of electricity expenditures will increase. While we can assume that the magnitude of this increase will be proportional to the biomass yield, productivity and nutrient composition of the crops in that area, the magnitude of this increase in the energy consumption at harvest will be largely dependent on the vitamin and protein contents of the alternative crops. For example, if every field in the area produces manure mixed with hematite, nitrates, or other ingredients, the new crop's high level of protein production could result in a large increase in the crop energy consumption at harvest, but not if all the fields in the area are relatively simple crops. So how important is energy consumption at harvest when calculating production costs? A systematic review of agricultural subsidies, published in 2004, that used data from many different sources showed that crop energy consumption at harvest is highly correlated with levels of crop productivity. While there was not enough evidence to prove a direct correlation from the electricity expenditures required to directly cultivate a field to the level of crop productivity, the authors found that crop energy consumption at harvest was on average more than 5 times as high as crop productivity was, in the range of 8 times the crop biomass energy required to produce each pound produced in a field. Furthermore, over multiple decades, the difference between crop and crop energy consumption at harvest increased (by one order of magnitude). Materials used to estimate agricultural energy expenditures at harvest in a systematic manner are limited, as they do not have a good representation for the large diversity of activities on a farm. There is, however, room for improvement. For example, one research team has explored building a spectrometer that can estimate energy expenditures. They managed to demonstrate that a 1% increase in the energy input required to move cattle from pasture to feed or slaughter facility on a typical American farm was enough to cause a 2% increase in the production costs. Due to the increase in energy used to move cattle to pasture, there was as much additional electricity from diesel generators to transport cattle to the barn to fill the new milking facilities for the pasture destructulation of the cattle industry, as it cost in electricity to move cows from the farm to slaughter. [4] Electricity expenditures at harvest probably are not as important compared to the expenditures that they could be replacing, such as when supplying electricity to oil and gas fields, or timber or ethane mines. More than two-thirds of US fresh"
